/* 生成的文件，不要手改：node tools/optics-build.mjs 从 tools/optics/ 生成（inputs 899e57c60616cab1，公式由 katex@0.16.11 转成 MathML）。 */
window.MOLASTRIA_OPTICS = {"index":{"title":"Optics","sub":"From Huygens’s wavelets to seeing atoms","eyebrow":"MOLASTRIA · WAVES, LIGHT AND IMAGES","h1":"Optics","question":"When light passes through two slits, why does the screen show a row of fringes rather than two bright lines?","lead":"Optics is the physics a chemistry laboratory uses most: behind every spectrometer, microscope and diffractometer sit a few principles from this section. It begins with a “wave tank”: every opening is a row of Huygens wavelet sources, and adding up their waves gives the patterns behind a single slit, a double slit, a grating, a round hole and an obstacle. Eight chapters cover waves and the Huygens–Fresnel principle, interference and coherence, diffraction, gratings and spectrometers, X-ray crystal diffraction, polarisation and interfaces, geometric optics, and finally Fourier optics, the limits of imaging and the frontier. All twelve demos are computed live in the browser.","scope":"<b>How to read the labels.</b> Every chapter and every key point carries a status: history, theorem (derived rigorously from Maxwell’s equations or the wave equation), established by experiment, approximation (holds under the stated conditions and fails outside them), frontier (under development; conclusions may change) and open. Many formulas in optics are approximations: Fraunhofer diffraction needs a distant screen, geometric optics needs openings much larger than the wavelength, the Scherrer formula holds only for small crystallites. The labels say which kind each is.","levels":{"history":["History","Documented history and facts about the literature"],"proved":["Theorem","Derived rigorously from Maxwell’s equations or the wave equation"],"tested":["Established by experiment","Supported by several independent experiments and in everyday use"],"approx":["Approximation","Holds under the stated conditions and fails outside them"],"frontier":["Frontier","Under development; conclusions and record numbers are still changing"],"open":["Open","Not yet achieved or proved by anyone"]},"demoKinds":{"exact":["Exact","Computed live from the definition or a closed formula (the model’s assumptions are listed under each demo)"]},"groups":[{"title":"The core of wave optics","ids":["waves","interference","diffraction","gratings"]},{"title":"Light and matter","ids":["xray","polarization"]},{"title":"Imaging","ids":["geometric","fourier"]}],"chapters":[{"id":"waves","n":"01","title":"Light is a wave: superposition and the Huygens–Fresnel principle","summary":"Light is an electromagnetic wave; wavelength, frequency, phase and complex amplitude; intensity is the square of the amplitude, so amplitudes add, not brightnesses. The Huygens–Fresnel principle turns diffraction and interference into “adding up wavelets”, and Kirchhoff gave it a mathematical form. The opening demo is the site owner Fe’s interactive diffraction animation.","level":"tested","demos":["tank"]},{"id":"interference","n":"02","title":"Interference and coherence: from Young’s slits to gravitational waves","summary":"The spacing of double-slit fringes; wavefront-splitting and amplitude-splitting interferometers; the colours of thin films, antireflection coatings and Newton’s rings; temporal and spatial coherence and the coherence length λ²/Δλ; Fourier-transform spectroscopy; single photons, electrons and C₆₀ molecules interfere too; LIGO and optical coherence tomography.","level":"tested","demos":["film","michelson"]},{"id":"diffraction","n":"03","title":"Diffraction: slits, round apertures, the Poisson spot and the limit of resolution","summary":"The Fraunhofer single-slit pattern and its minima; Fresnel diffraction, the Cornu spiral and the straight edge; why the Fresnel number decides which one you see; the Airy disc of a round aperture, the Rayleigh criterion and the resolution of eyes, telescopes and microscopes; the story of the Poisson spot, Babinet’s principle, zone plates and “diffraction-free” Bessel beams.","level":"tested","demos":["fresnel","airy"]},{"id":"gratings","n":"04","title":"Many slits and gratings: splitting light into a spectrum","summary":"The interference factor of N slits, principal and subsidiary maxima; the grating equation, angular dispersion and resolving power R = mN; free spectral range, blazed and echelle gratings; Fraunhofer, Rowland and ruling engines; prisms versus gratings; chirped-pulse amplification; structural colour on discs and butterfly wings; how spectrometers are used in chemistry.","level":"tested","demos":["grating"]},{"id":"xray","n":"05","title":"X-rays and crystal diffraction: seeing atoms with the logic of gratings","summary":"The Laue photograph of 1912 and the Braggs’ law of reflection; a three-dimensional grating, the reciprocal lattice, the Laue condition and the Ewald sphere; structure factors and systematic absences; powder diffraction, the Scherrer formula and Rietveld refinement; the phase problem and the Patterson function; the DNA fibre pattern, myoglobin and quasicrystals; X-ray free-electron lasers and coherent diffraction imaging.","level":"tested","demos":["bragg","powder"]},{"id":"polarization","n":"06","title":"Polarisation and interfaces: light is a transverse wave","summary":"The electric field of light is perpendicular to its direction of travel and can oscillate in different directions; Fresnel’s equations give how much is reflected and transmitted at an interface; Brewster’s angle and total internal reflection; Jones vectors and matrices, polarisers and wave plates; circular polarisation and chiral molecules, optical rotation and circular dichroism; why the sky is blue and polarised, Rayleigh and Mie scattering; birefringence and stress.","level":"proved","demos":["reflect","jones"]},{"id":"geometric","n":"07","title":"Geometric optics: rays in the limit of vanishing wavelength","summary":"Fermat’s principle and Snell’s law; geometric optics as the short-wavelength limit of wave optics; lenses, the imaging equation and the lensmaker’s formula; the aberrations of real lenses: spherical, chromatic and the rest; numerical aperture; total internal reflection and loss in optical fibres; optical tweezers that hold small particles with the momentum of light.","level":"approx","demos":["lens"]},{"id":"fourier","n":"08","title":"Fourier optics, imaging and the frontier","summary":"A lens performs a Fourier transform: Abbe’s theory of microscope imaging and the resolution limit; spatial filtering, dark field and Zernike phase contrast; holography; the fast Fourier transform brings all this into the computer; super-resolution fluorescence microscopy (STED, PALM, STORM) that gets round the diffraction limit; coherent diffraction imaging; negative refraction and metamaterials, photonic crystals, light with orbital angular momentum; and the questions still open.","level":"tested","demos":["abbe"]}],"milestones":[{"year":"1690","text":"Huygens’s Treatise on Light: every point of a wavefront is a new source of wavelets (<a href=\"#c-waves\" data-open=\"waves\">01 · Light is a wave: superposition and the Huygens–Fresnel principle</a>)."},{"year":"1804","text":"Young argues from interference fringes (of slits, threads and hairs) that light is a wave <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-young1804\" data-ref=\"young1804\">Young 1804</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1815","text":"Brewster finds the angle at which reflected light is completely polarised <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-brewster1815\" data-ref=\"brewster1815\">Brewster 1815</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1818","text":"Fresnel’s memoir on diffraction; Poisson deduces a bright spot at the centre of a disc’s shadow as an objection, and Arago promptly observes it (<a href=\"#c-waves\" data-open=\"waves\">01 · Light is a wave: superposition and the Huygens–Fresnel principle</a>)."},{"year":"1871","text":"Rayleigh explains the blue sky with 1/λ⁴ scattering <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rayleigh1871\" data-ref=\"rayleigh1871\">Rayleigh 1871</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1873","text":"Abbe’s theory of microscope imaging and its resolution limit <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-abbe1873\" data-ref=\"abbe1873\">Abbe 1873</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1879","text":"Rayleigh’s criterion of resolution and the resolving power of gratings <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rayleigh1879\" data-ref=\"rayleigh1879\">Rayleigh 1879</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1882","text":"Rowland’s concave grating and ruling engine <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rowland1882\" data-ref=\"rowland1882\">Rowland 1882</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1883","text":"Kirchhoff gives the Huygens–Fresnel principle a mathematical form <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kirchhoff1883\" data-ref=\"kirchhoff1883\">Kirchhoff 1883</a>]</span>; in 1896 Sommerfeld solves diffraction by a half-plane exactly <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-sommerfeld1896\" data-ref=\"sommerfeld1896\">Sommerfeld 1896</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1887","text":"Michelson and Morley’s interferometer finds no “ether wind” <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-michelson1887\" data-ref=\"michelson1887\">Michelson &amp; Morley 1887</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1908","text":"Mie solves scattering by a sphere and explains the colour of colloidal gold <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-mie1908\" data-ref=\"mie1908\">Mie 1908</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1913","text":"The X-ray diffraction photograph of Friedrich, Knipping and Laue <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-laue1913\" data-ref=\"laue1913\">Friedrich, Knipping &amp; Laue 1913</a>]</span>; Bragg’s law of reflection <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bragg1913\" data-ref=\"bragg1913\">Bragg &amp; Bragg 1913</a>]</span> (<a href=\"#c-xray\" data-open=\"xray\">05 · X-rays and crystal diffraction: seeing atoms with the logic of gratings</a>)."},{"year":"1934","text":"The Patterson function: interatomic vectors without phases <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-patterson1934\" data-ref=\"patterson1934\">Patterson 1934</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1938","text":"Zernike defines the degree of coherence <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zernike1938\" data-ref=\"zernike1938\">Zernike 1938</a>]</span>; in 1942 he publishes the phase-contrast microscope <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zernike1942\" data-ref=\"zernike1942\">Zernike 1942</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1941","text":"Jones’s matrix calculus for polarisation <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-jones1941\" data-ref=\"jones1941\">Jones 1941</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1948","text":"Gabor invents holography <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gabor1948\" data-ref=\"gabor1948\">Gabor 1948</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1953","text":"Franklin and Gosling’s fibre diffraction pattern of DNA <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-franklin1953\" data-ref=\"franklin1953\">Franklin &amp; Gosling 1953</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1956","text":"Hanbury Brown and Twiss measure correlations of intensity <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hbt1956\" data-ref=\"hbt1956\">Hanbury Brown &amp; Twiss 1956</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1958","text":"The first three-dimensional structure of a protein, myoglobin <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kendrew1958\" data-ref=\"kendrew1958\">Kendrew et al. 1958</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1960","text":"Maiman’s ruby laser <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-maiman1960\" data-ref=\"maiman1960\">Maiman 1960</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1961","text":"Jönsson’s electron double-slit interference <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-jonsson1961\" data-ref=\"jonsson1961\">Jönsson 1961</a>]</span>; in 1989 Tonomura watches the fringes build up one electron at a time <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-tonomura1989\" data-ref=\"tonomura1989\">Tonomura et al. 1989</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1965","text":"Cooley and Tukey’s fast Fourier transform <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-cooley1965\" data-ref=\"cooley1965\">Cooley &amp; Tukey 1965</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1966","text":"Kao and Hockham show that fibre loss comes from impurities <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kao1966\" data-ref=\"kao1966\">Kao &amp; Hockham 1966</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1969","text":"Rietveld fits the whole powder pattern with a structural model <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rietveld1969\" data-ref=\"rietveld1969\">Rietveld 1969</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1984","text":"Shechtman’s tenfold diffraction pattern: quasicrystals <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-shechtman1984\" data-ref=\"shechtman1984\">Shechtman et al. 1984</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1985","text":"Strickland and Mourou’s chirped-pulse amplification <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-strickland1985\" data-ref=\"strickland1985\">Strickland &amp; Mourou 1985</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1986","text":"Ashkin’s single-beam optical tweezers <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ashkin1986\" data-ref=\"ashkin1986\">Ashkin et al. 1986</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1987","text":"Photonic crystals proposed <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-yablonovitch1987\" data-ref=\"yablonovitch1987\">Yablonovitch 1987</a>; <a href=\"#ref-john1987\" data-ref=\"john1987\">John 1987</a>]</span>; diffraction-free Bessel beams <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-durnin1987\" data-ref=\"durnin1987\">Durnin et al. 1987</a>]</span>; Hong–Ou–Mandel two-photon interference <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hom1987\" data-ref=\"hom1987\">Hong et al. 1987</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1991","text":"Optical coherence tomography (OCT) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-huang1991\" data-ref=\"huang1991\">Huang et al. 1991</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1992","text":"Allen and colleagues: light can carry orbital angular momentum <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-allen1992\" data-ref=\"allen1992\">Allen et al. 1992</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1994","text":"Hell and Wichmann propose STED super-resolution microscopy <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hell1994\" data-ref=\"hell1994\">Hell &amp; Wichmann 1994</a>]</span>."},{"year":"1999","text":"Interference of C₆₀ molecules <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-arndt1999\" data-ref=\"arndt1999\">Arndt et al. 1999</a>]</span>; coherent diffraction imaging <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-miao1999\" data-ref=\"miao1999\">Miao et al. 1999</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2000","text":"Simulations of “diffraction before destruction” <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-neutze2000\" data-ref=\"neutze2000\">Neutze et al. 2000</a>]</span>; Pendry’s perfect lens <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-pendry2000\" data-ref=\"pendry2000\">Pendry 2000</a>]</span>; negative refraction at microwave frequencies in 2001 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-shelby2001\" data-ref=\"shelby2001\">Shelby et al. 2001</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2006","text":"PALM <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-betzig2006\" data-ref=\"betzig2006\">Betzig et al. 2006</a>]</span> and STORM <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rust2006\" data-ref=\"rust2006\">Rust et al. 2006</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2011","text":"Femtosecond nanocrystallography at an X-ray free-electron laser <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chapman2011\" data-ref=\"chapman2011\">Chapman et al. 2011</a>]</span>."},{"year":"2016","text":"LIGO’s interferometers detect gravitational waves <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ligo2016\" data-ref=\"ligo2016\">LIGO &amp; Virgo 2016</a>]</span>."}],"quiz":[{"q":"A disc blocks a parallel beam of light. What is at the very centre of its shadow?","opts":["The darkest point: all the light is blocked","A bright spot: every point of the rim is the same optical distance from the centre, so the wavelets arrive in phase","It depends on the colour of the disc","Only laser light gives a spot; sunlight never does"],"a":1,"why":"This is the Poisson–Arago spot. It needs enough coherence, which sunlight through a pinhole provides. Choose “obstacle” in the wave-tank demo to see it.","ch":"waves"},{"q":"A helium–neon laser (632.8 nm) falls on a double slit with spacing 0.25 mm. What is the fringe spacing on a screen 1 m away?","opts":["0.25 mm","About 2.5 mm","About 25 mm","About 0.63 mm"],"a":1,"why":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>632.8</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.25</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2.53</mn></mrow></semantics></math> mm.","ch":"interference"},{"q":"An LED has a centre wavelength of 600 nm and a linewidth of 30 nm. Roughly what is its coherence length?","opts":["About 12 μm","About 1 mm","About 30 nm","About 1 m"],"a":0,"why":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>600</mn><mtext> nm</mtext><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>30</mn><mtext> nm</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></semantics></math> μm. So an LED shows no interference from a thick glass plate; only very thin films (soap bubbles) show colours.","ch":"interference"},{"q":"If a single slit is made half as wide, what happens to the central bright band on the screen?","opts":["It becomes half as wide","Same width, just dimmer","It becomes twice as wide","It splits in two"],"a":2,"why":"The first dark fringe is at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; halving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> doubles the angle. The narrower the slit, the more the light spreads: the “uncertainty” of the Fourier transform.","ch":"diffraction"},{"q":"Roughly what is the smallest angular separation a 10 cm telescope can resolve at 550 nm?","opts":["About 0.014″","About 1.4″","About 14″","About 1′"],"a":1,"why":"The Rayleigh criterion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.22</mn><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.22</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>550</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.1</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>6.7</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> rad <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1.38</mn><mtext>″</mtext></mrow></semantics></math>.","ch":"diffraction"},{"q":"To separate the two sodium D lines (589.0 nm and 589.6 nm) in the first order, at least how many grating lines must be illuminated?","opts":["About 100","About 1000","About 10 000","The number of lines does not matter, only their density"],"a":1,"why":"The resolving power <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>589.3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>982</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow></semantics></math> in the first order.","ch":"gratings"},{"q":"Why does face-centred cubic copper have no (100) or (110) diffraction peak?","opts":["There are no atoms on those planes","The scattering of the four atoms in the cell cancels exactly in those directions: F = 0 when h, k, l are mixed odd and even","The X-ray wavelength is too long","Copper absorbs X-rays in those directions"],"a":1,"why":"This is the systematic absence of a face-centred lattice; copper’s first peak is (111), at 43.3° with Cu Kα.","ch":"xray"},{"q":"Put a polariser with its axis at 45° between two crossed polarisers. How much unpolarised light gets through?","opts":["0: two crossed polarisers always block everything","1/2","1/4","1/8"],"a":3,"why":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>45</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi><mo>×</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>45</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. The middle polariser projects the polarisation onto 45°, and the third projects again.","ch":"polarization"},{"q":"Why do polarised sunglasses cut the glare from water?","opts":["The lenses absorb all blue light","Near Brewster’s angle the reflection from water is almost entirely horizontally polarised, and the lenses pass only vertical polarisation","Glare has a longer wavelength than direct light","The lenses have an antireflection coating"],"a":1,"why":"The Brewster angle from air to water is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>arctan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>1.333</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>53.1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; near it the p (vertical) polarisation is hardly reflected.","ch":"polarization"},{"q":"Focusing a parallel beam with a plano-convex lens, which side should face the light for less spherical aberration?","opts":["The flat side","The curved side","It makes no difference","It depends on the colour of the glass"],"a":1,"why":"With the curved side to the beam the two surfaces share the bending, each with smaller angles of incidence, and the aberration is about four times smaller (compare in the ray-tracing demo).","ch":"geometric"},{"q":"A microscope with coherent 550 nm light and an NA 0.4 objective looks at a grating of period 1.2 μm. Are fringes visible in the image?","opts":["Yes, just a little blurred","No: the first order’s sine of 0.46 exceeds the NA, only the zeroth order enters, and the image is a uniform glow","Yes, but the colour changes","It depends on the grating material"],"a":1,"why":"The Abbe limit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1.375</mn></mrow></semantics></math> μm is larger than 1.2 μm. Oblique illumination or an oil objective pushes the limit lower.","ch":"fourier"},{"q":"Did super-resolution microscopes such as STED and PALM overturn Abbe’s diffraction limit?","opts":["Yes: the diffraction spot can be made arbitrarily small","No: the diffraction spot is as large as ever; they make neighbouring molecules emit at different times and localise them one by one","No, they simply use shorter wavelengths","Yes, because they use quantum entanglement"],"a":1,"why":"The centre of an isolated spot can be located to about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>; the key is switching the fluorescent molecules on and off.","ch":"fourier"}],"books":[{"title":"Optics","authors":"Eugene Hecht (Pearson, 5th ed. 2017)","note":"The most widely used undergraduate optics textbook, richly illustrated, from waves and geometry to Fourier optics."},{"title":"Principles of Optics","authors":"Max Born, Emil Wolf (Cambridge University Press, 7th ed. 1999)","note":"The standard reference of classical optics, with full derivations of diffraction, coherence and polarisation."},{"title":"Introduction to Fourier Optics","authors":"Joseph W. Goodman (W. H. Freeman, 4th ed. 2017)","note":"Chapter 8 at full length: diffraction integrals, the Fourier-transforming lens, imaging systems and holography."},{"title":"Fundamentals of Photonics","authors":"Bahaa E. A. Saleh, Malvin Carl Teich (Wiley, 3rd ed. 2019)","note":"From rays, waves and electromagnetism to photons, plus fibres, lasers and photonic crystals, with an engineering slant."},{"title":"Elements of Modern X-ray Physics","authors":"Jens Als-Nielsen, Des McMorrow (Wiley, 2nd ed. 2011)","note":"Chapter 5 at full length: scattering, crystallography, coherent diffraction and synchrotron radiation, with a physical approach."},{"title":"The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Vol. I, chapters 26–33","authors":"R. P. Feynman, R. B. Leighton, M. Sands (Caltech, free online)","note":"From the principle of least time to interference, diffraction, the origin of the refractive index and polarisation; a good first read."}],"open":[{"q":"Can low-loss negative-index materials and a real “perfect lens” be made at visible wavelengths?","ch":"fourier"},{"q":"Can the atomic structure of a single protein molecule be determined with an X-ray laser, without a crystal?","ch":"xray"},{"q":"Can super-resolution microscopy be fast, gentle on living cells and molecular in resolution all at once?","ch":"fourier"},{"q":"How far can photonic crystals and metasurfaces replace conventional lenses and prisms?","ch":"fourier"},{"q":"How should the (position-space) wavefunction of a single photon be defined? The photon has no position operator in the usual sense, and this is still debated.","ch":"waves"}],"notes":{"map":"Each card is a chapter. The label gives the status of the bulk of the chapter; key points inside carry their own labels. Chapters you have opened are ticked (on this device only).","history":"Years are publication years.","path":"To read on: the Spectra Hall’s <a href=\"../spectra/\">91 analytical methods</a> are these principles at work in the laboratory; the chapter <a href=\"../chemistry/structural/#c-diffraction\">Diffraction and structure solution</a> in the Chemistry Disciplines explains how crystal structures are solved; the Quantum Physics pavilion’s <a href=\"../quantum/physics/#c-optics\">quantum optics</a> chapter covers photons; and in the Playground the <a href=\"../qchem/#play-wave\">2D wave-packet wall</a> lets you draw your own slits and watch a quantum wave diffract.","library":"46 references. Each was looked up by DOI in OpenAlex (which draws on Crossref) for title, authors, year and volume (November 2026; node tools/optics-refs.mjs re-checks them)."}},"refs":[{"id":"young1804","authors":"Thomas Young","year":1804,"title":"The Bakerian Lecture: Experiments and calculations relative to physical optics","journal":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London","vol":"94","pages":"1–16","doi":"10.1098/rstl.1804.0001","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["waves","interference"]},{"id":"brewster1815","authors":"David Brewster","year":1815,"title":"On the laws which regulate the polarisation of light by reflexion from transparent bodies","journal":"Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society of London","vol":"105","pages":"125–159","doi":"10.1098/rstl.1815.0010","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["polarization"]},{"id":"rayleigh1871","authors":"J. W. Strutt (Lord Rayleigh)","year":1871,"title":"On the light from the sky, its polarization and colour","journal":"The London, Edinburgh, and Dublin Philosophical Magazine and Journal of Science","vol":"41","pages":"107–120","doi":"10.1080/14786447108640452","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["polarization"]},{"id":"abbe1873","authors":"E. Abbe","year":1873,"title":"Beiträge zur Theorie des Mikroskops und der mikroskopischen Wahrnehmung","journal":"Archiv für Mikroskopische Anatomie","vol":"9","pages":"413–468","doi":"10.1007/BF02956173","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fourier"]},{"id":"rayleigh1879","authors":"Lord Rayleigh","year":1879,"title":"Investigations in optics, with special reference to the spectroscope","journal":"The London, Edinburgh, and Dublin Philosophical Magazine and Journal of Science","vol":"8","pages":"261–274","doi":"10.1080/14786447908639684","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["diffraction","gratings"]},{"id":"rowland1882","authors":"H. A. Rowland","year":1882,"title":"Preliminary notice of the results accomplished in the manufacture and theory of gratings for optical purposes","journal":"The London, Edinburgh, and Dublin Philosophical Magazine and Journal of Science","vol":"13","pages":"469–474","doi":"10.1080/14786448208627217","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["gratings"]},{"id":"kirchhoff1883","authors":"G. Kirchhoff","year":1883,"title":"Zur Theorie der Lichtstrahlen","journal":"Annalen der Physik","vol":"254","pages":"663–695","doi":"10.1002/andp.18832540409","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["waves"]},{"id":"michelson1887","authors":"A. A. Michelson, E. W. Morley","year":1887,"title":"On the relative motion of the Earth and the luminiferous ether","journal":"American Journal of Science","vol":"34","pages":"333–345","doi":"10.2475/ajs.s3-34.203.333","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["interference"]},{"id":"sommerfeld1896","authors":"A. Sommerfeld","year":1896,"title":"Mathematische Theorie der Diffraction","journal":"Mathematische Annalen","vol":"47","pages":"317–374","doi":"10.1007/BF01447273","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["waves","diffraction"]},{"id":"mie1908","authors":"Gustav Mie","year":1908,"title":"Beiträge zur Optik trüber Medien, speziell kolloidaler Metallösungen","journal":"Annalen der Physik","vol":"330","pages":"377–445","doi":"10.1002/andp.19083300302","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["polarization"]},{"id":"bragg1913","authors":"William Henry Bragg, William Lawrence Bragg","year":1913,"title":"The reflection of X-rays by crystals","journal":"Proceedings of the Royal Society of London A","vol":"88","pages":"428–438","doi":"10.1098/rspa.1913.0040","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["xray"]},{"id":"laue1913","authors":"W. Friedrich, P. Knipping, M. Laue","year":1913,"title":"Interferenzerscheinungen bei Röntgenstrahlen","journal":"Annalen der Physik","vol":"346","pages":"971–988","doi":"10.1002/andp.19133461004","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["xray"]},{"id":"patterson1934","authors":"A. L. Patterson","year":1934,"title":"A Fourier Series Method for the Determination of the Components of Interatomic Distances in Crystals","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"46","pages":"372–376","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.46.372","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["xray"]},{"id":"zernike1938","authors":"F. Zernike","year":1938,"title":"The concept of degree of coherence and its application to optical problems","journal":"Physica","vol":"5","pages":"785–795","doi":"10.1016/S0031-8914(38)80203-2","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["interference"]},{"id":"patterson1939","authors":"A. L. Patterson","year":1939,"title":"The Scherrer Formula for X-Ray Particle Size Determination","journal":"Physical Review","vol":"56","pages":"978–982","doi":"10.1103/PhysRev.56.978","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["xray"]},{"id":"jones1941","authors":"R. Clark Jones","year":1941,"title":"A New Calculus for the Treatment of Optical Systems. I. Description and Discussion of the Calculus","journal":"Journal of the Optical Society of America","vol":"31","pages":"488–493","doi":"10.1364/JOSA.31.000488","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["polarization"]},{"id":"zernike1942","authors":"F. Zernike","year":1942,"title":"Phase contrast, a new method for the microscopic observation of transparent objects","journal":"Physica","vol":"9","pages":"686–698","doi":"10.1016/S0031-8914(42)80035-X","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fourier"]},{"id":"gabor1948","authors":"D. Gabor","year":1948,"title":"A New Microscopic Principle","journal":"Nature","vol":"161","pages":"777–778","doi":"10.1038/161777a0","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fourier"]},{"id":"franklin1953","authors":"Rosalind E. Franklin, R. G. Gosling","year":1953,"title":"Molecular Configuration in Sodium Thymonucleate","journal":"Nature","vol":"171","pages":"740–741","doi":"10.1038/171740a0","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["xray"]},{"id":"hbt1956","authors":"R. Hanbury Brown, R. Q. Twiss","year":1956,"title":"Correlation between Photons in two Coherent Beams of Light","journal":"Nature","vol":"177","pages":"27–29","doi":"10.1038/177027a0","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["interference"]},{"id":"kendrew1958","authors":"J. C. Kendrew et al.","year":1958,"title":"A Three-Dimensional Model of the Myoglobin Molecule Obtained by X-Ray Analysis","journal":"Nature","vol":"181","pages":"662–666","doi":"10.1038/181662a0","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["xray"]},{"id":"maiman1960","authors":"T. H. Maiman","year":1960,"title":"Stimulated Optical Radiation in Ruby","journal":"Nature","vol":"187","pages":"493–494","doi":"10.1038/187493a0","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["waves"]},{"id":"jonsson1961","authors":"Claus Jönsson","year":1961,"title":"Elektroneninterferenzen an mehreren künstlich hergestellten Feinspalten","journal":"Zeitschrift für Physik","vol":"161","pages":"454–474","doi":"10.1007/BF01342460","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["interference"]},{"id":"cooley1965","authors":"James W. Cooley, John W. Tukey","year":1965,"title":"An Algorithm for the Machine Calculation of Complex Fourier Series","journal":"Mathematics of Computation","vol":"19","pages":"297–301","doi":"10.1090/S0025-5718-1965-0178586-1","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fourier"]},{"id":"kao1966","authors":"K. C. Kao, G. A. Hockham","year":1966,"title":"Dielectric-fibre surface waveguides for optical frequencies","journal":"Proceedings of the Institution of Electrical Engineers","vol":"113","pages":"1151–1158","doi":"10.1049/piee.1966.0189","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["geometric"]},{"id":"rietveld1969","authors":"H. M. Rietveld","year":1969,"title":"A profile refinement method for nuclear and magnetic structures","journal":"Journal of Applied Crystallography","vol":"2","pages":"65–71","doi":"10.1107/S0021889869006558","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["xray"]},{"id":"shechtman1984","authors":"D. Shechtman et al.","year":1984,"title":"Metallic Phase with Long-Range Orientational Order and No Translational Symmetry","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"53","pages":"1951–1953","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.53.1951","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["xray"]},{"id":"strickland1985","authors":"Donna Strickland, Gerard Mourou","year":1985,"title":"Compression of amplified chirped optical pulses","journal":"Optics Communications","vol":"56","pages":"219–221","doi":"10.1016/0030-4018(85)90120-8","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["gratings"]},{"id":"ashkin1986","authors":"A. Ashkin et al.","year":1986,"title":"Observation of a single-beam gradient force optical trap for dielectric particles","journal":"Optics Letters","vol":"11","pages":"288–290","doi":"10.1364/OL.11.000288","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["geometric"]},{"id":"durnin1987","authors":"J. Durnin, J. J. Miceli, J. H. Eberly","year":1987,"title":"Diffraction-free beams","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"58","pages":"1499–1501","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.58.1499","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["diffraction"]},{"id":"hom1987","authors":"C. K. Hong, Z. Y. Ou, L. Mandel","year":1987,"title":"Measurement of subpicosecond time intervals between two photons by interference","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"59","pages":"2044–2046","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.59.2044","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["interference"]},{"id":"john1987","authors":"Sajeev John","year":1987,"title":"Strong localization of photons in certain disordered dielectric superlattices","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"58","pages":"2486–2489","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.58.2486","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fourier"]},{"id":"yablonovitch1987","authors":"Eli Yablonovitch","year":1987,"title":"Inhibited Spontaneous Emission in Solid-State Physics and Electronics","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"58","pages":"2059–2062","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.58.2059","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fourier"]},{"id":"tonomura1989","authors":"A. Tonomura et al.","year":1989,"title":"Demonstration of single-electron buildup of an interference pattern","journal":"American Journal of Physics","vol":"57","pages":"117–120","doi":"10.1119/1.16104","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["interference"]},{"id":"huang1991","authors":"David Huang et al.","year":1991,"title":"Optical Coherence Tomography","journal":"Science","vol":"254","pages":"1178–1181","doi":"10.1126/science.1957169","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["interference"]},{"id":"allen1992","authors":"L. Allen et al.","year":1992,"title":"Orbital angular momentum of light and the transformation of Laguerre-Gaussian laser modes","journal":"Physical Review A","vol":"45","pages":"8185–8189","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevA.45.8185","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fourier"]},{"id":"hell1994","authors":"Stefan W. Hell, Jan Wichmann","year":1994,"title":"Breaking the diffraction resolution limit by stimulated emission: stimulated-emission-depletion fluorescence microscopy","journal":"Optics Letters","vol":"19","pages":"780–782","doi":"10.1364/OL.19.000780","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fourier"]},{"id":"arndt1999","authors":"Markus Arndt et al.","year":1999,"title":"Wave–particle duality of C60 molecules","journal":"Nature","vol":"401","pages":"680–682","doi":"10.1038/44348","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["interference"]},{"id":"miao1999","authors":"Jianwei Miao et al.","year":1999,"title":"Extending the methodology of X-ray crystallography to allow imaging of micrometre-sized non-crystalline specimens","journal":"Nature","vol":"400","pages":"342–344","doi":"10.1038/22498","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["xray","fourier"]},{"id":"neutze2000","authors":"Richard Neutze et al.","year":2000,"title":"Potential for biomolecular imaging with femtosecond X-ray pulses","journal":"Nature","vol":"406","pages":"752–757","doi":"10.1038/35021099","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["xray"]},{"id":"pendry2000","authors":"J. B. Pendry","year":2000,"title":"Negative Refraction Makes a Perfect Lens","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"85","pages":"3966–3969","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.85.3966","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fourier"]},{"id":"shelby2001","authors":"R. A. Shelby, D. R. Smith, S. Schultz","year":2001,"title":"Experimental Verification of a Negative Index of Refraction","journal":"Science","vol":"292","pages":"77–79","doi":"10.1126/science.1058847","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fourier"]},{"id":"betzig2006","authors":"Eric Betzig et al.","year":2006,"title":"Imaging Intracellular Fluorescent Proteins at Nanometer Resolution","journal":"Science","vol":"313","pages":"1642–1645","doi":"10.1126/science.1127344","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fourier"]},{"id":"rust2006","authors":"Michael J. Rust, Mark Bates, Xiaowei Zhuang","year":2006,"title":"Sub-diffraction-limit imaging by stochastic optical reconstruction microscopy (STORM)","journal":"Nature Methods","vol":"3","pages":"793–796","doi":"10.1038/nmeth929","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["fourier"]},{"id":"chapman2011","authors":"Henry N. Chapman et al.","year":2011,"title":"Femtosecond X-ray protein nanocrystallography","journal":"Nature","vol":"470","pages":"73–77","doi":"10.1038/nature09750","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["xray"]},{"id":"ligo2016","authors":"B. P. Abbott et al. (LIGO Scientific Collaboration and Virgo Collaboration)","year":2016,"title":"Observation of Gravitational Waves from a Binary Black Hole Merger","journal":"Physical Review Letters","vol":"116","pages":"061102","doi":"10.1103/PhysRevLett.116.061102","arxiv":"","url":"","note":"","used":["interference"]}],"chapters":{"waves":{"id":"waves","body":[{"p":"Nearly every “strange” effect in optics, diffraction, interference, polarisation, grating spectra, X-ray crystallography and holography, comes from one fact: light is a wave, and waves superpose. This chapter sets out the language of waves and then introduces a method that runs through the whole section: treat every point of a wavefront as a new source and add up the wavelets it emits. The wave tank below is computed exactly that way."},{"demo":"tank"},{"h":"Light is an electromagnetic wave"},{"p":"In vacuum Maxwell’s equations give a wave equation for the electric and magnetic fields, travelling at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>299</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>792</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>458</mn></mrow></semantics></math> m/s. Visible light has wavelengths <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> of roughly 380 to 750 nm and frequencies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> of 400 to 790 THz; X-rays are a thousand to ten thousand times shorter (about 0.01 to 10 nm), and the infrared and microwaves much longer. They are the same kind of wave on different scales. In a medium light slows to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is the refractive index; the frequency stays the same, so the wavelength shrinks to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"p":"For many problems one can forget for a while that the electric field is a vector and let a single scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></semantics></math> stand for one field component. This is the scalar-wave approximation, used by the diffraction theory of chapter 3 and by the demo above. What it leaves out (polarisation, the electromagnetic conditions at boundaries) comes back in chapter 6."},{"h":"Phase, complex amplitude and intensity"},{"p":"A monochromatic plane wave is written <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, with wavenumber <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and angular frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. The whole angle in the bracket is the phase. A more convenient form for calculation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>U</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> is the complex amplitude: its modulus is the amplitude, its argument the phase. Two waves of the same frequency superpose by adding their complex amplitudes, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"p":"Eyes, cameras and photodetectors are far too slow to follow oscillations at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>15</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> Hz; they measure the time-averaged energy flow, the intensity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∝</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>U</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>φ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. The last term is the interference term: for two equally bright beams the brightness is four times that of one when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and zero when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Light plus light can give darkness; that sentence is the whole secret of interference."},{"p":"A phase difference usually comes from an optical path difference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. A geometric length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> in a medium is equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> in vacuum, so the optical path is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. The “path difference” readout in the demo is the difference between the distances from the chosen point to the two slits (or the two edges of one slit), in wavelengths."},{"h":"Huygens, Fresnel and Kirchhoff"},{"p":"In 1690 Huygens proposed that every point of a wavefront is a secondary source and that the wavefront a moment later is the envelope of these wavelets. That explains reflection and refraction, but not why light mostly travels in straight lines or why there are dark fringes. In 1818 Fresnel supplied the key step: the wavelets do not merely form an envelope, they add with their own phases and interfere. Straight-line travel is then the result of wavelets cancelling in other directions, and diffraction fringes the result of incomplete cancellation."},{"p":"Before that, in his Bakerian Lecture of 1804 Young had already argued for the wave nature of light from the interference fringes of two small openings and had estimated the wavelengths of the colours from the fringe spacing <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-young1804\" data-ref=\"young1804\">Young 1804</a>]</span>. In 1882–83 Kirchhoff started from the wave equation and Green’s theorem and wrote Fresnel’s idea as an integral <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kirchhoff1883\" data-ref=\"kirchhoff1883\">Kirchhoff 1883</a>]</span>: in the openings the field is taken to be the incident wave, behind the screen zero, and each point contributes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></semantics></math> times an obliquity factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. That is exactly the sum in the demo."},{"note":"<b>Kirchhoff’s boundary conditions contradict each other.</b> Requiring both the field and its normal derivative to vanish on the screen implies, mathematically, that the field vanishes everywhere. The method works because the error is small when the openings are many wavelengths wide. In 1896 Sommerfeld solved diffraction exactly for an infinitely thin, perfectly conducting half-plane <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-sommerfeld1896\" data-ref=\"sommerfeld1896\">Sommerfeld 1896</a>]</span>, one of the few exact solutions; today, when an opening is close to a wavelength wide, one simply solves Maxwell’s equations numerically.","kind":"warn"},{"h":"What to look for in the tank"},{"list":["<b>A narrow slit (a &lt; λ)</b>: the slit is effectively a single source and what leaves it is almost a circular wave going in every direction. This is diffraction in its purest form.","<b>A wide slit (a ≫ λ)</b>: across the middle the wavelets are in phase and form a straight beam with a sharp geometric shadow; only near the edges are there fine fringes. Geometric optics is this limit (chapter 7).","<b>A double slit</b>: where the two diffracted beams overlap there are bright and dark fringes spaced by λ/d (in sin θ). This is the subject of chapter 2.","<b>Many slits</b>: the more slits, the narrower and brighter the bright fringes and the weaker the subsidiary maxima between them; this is how a grating separates colours (chapter 4).","<b>An obstacle</b>: in the very centre of what should geometrically be the darkest shadow there is a bright line. In three dimensions, the bright spot behind a disc is called the Poisson spot (chapter 3)."]},{"p":"The phasor diagram turns superposition into geometry: each piece of the wavelets is a small arrow whose length is its amplitude and whose direction is its phase; laid head to tail, the arrow from start to finish is the resultant. At a dark fringe the small arrows close into a circle; at a bright fringe they line up almost straight. Many results can be read straight off this picture, such as the first dark fringe of a single slit: when the path difference between the two edges is exactly one wavelength, the arrows go round exactly once."},{"h":"Near field and far field"},{"p":"Close to the slit, the differences in distance to different points of the slit are not a simple function of angle; the pattern changes with distance, and this is Fresnel diffraction. Far enough away (or in the focal plane of a lens) each point corresponds to a single direction and the pattern depends only on angle: Fraunhofer diffraction. The boundary is set by the Fresnel number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the half-width of the opening and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the distance. The tank in the demo is only 14 μm long and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is not small, so the solid curve on the screen does not coincide with the far-field formula (dashed); tick “lens focal plane” to see the clean far-field pattern. Chapter 3 is about this transition."},{"p":"Diffraction and interference are really the same thing: both add up the complex amplitudes of wavelets. By convention, superposing a few beams (two or several) is called interference and superposing a continuum of wavelets diffraction. Feynman remarked in his lectures that nobody has ever managed to define the difference between them satisfactorily."},{"h":"Coherence and the laser"},{"p":"For interference fringes to be steady, the phase difference between two beams must stay constant over the measurement. In an ordinary source countless atoms emit independently and start afresh every few nanoseconds, so the phase difference between two lamps jumps about and the fringes flicker past unseen. That is why Young sent one beam through two small openings: both paths come from the same wavefront and their phase difference is fixed. In 1960 Maiman built the first (ruby) laser <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-maiman1960\" data-ref=\"maiman1960\">Maiman 1960</a>]</span>, and from then on there were sources that are monochromatic, highly directional and coherent over metres or even kilometres; interference became an everyday tool. Coherence is made quantitative in chapter 2."}],"points":[{"h":"Complex amplitudes add; intensities are what is measured","p":"The cross term in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> lets “light plus light” become darker. As long as the medium is linear (the light not too intense), superposition holds exactly.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The Huygens–Fresnel principle","p":"Treating every point of a wavefront as a source of wavelets and adding them with their phases gives diffraction and interference. It is an integral representation that follows from the wave equation, not an extra assumption.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Kirchhoff’s approximation","p":"Take the incident field in the openings and zero behind the screen, with obliquity factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. Small errors when openings are much wider than the wavelength; near a wavelength, solve Maxwell’s equations.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Diffraction and interference are the same thing","p":"Both are superpositions of wavelets; a few beams are called interference and a continuum diffraction, by convention only.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The Fresnel number decides near or far field","p":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≫</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: geometric shadow plus edge fringes; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: the Fraunhofer pattern, a function of angle only.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Young 1804: light is a wave","p":"Fringes from two openings, together with wavelengths estimated from their spacing, were among the first quantitative evidence for the wave theory of light.","st":"history"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>u</mi><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>u</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∂</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ω</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∇</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>U</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The scalar wave equation and its monochromatic form (the Helmholtz equation), k = 2π/λ."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mtext> </mtext><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>φ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Interference of two beams; Δ is the optical path difference."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>∬</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">A</mi></msub><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Kirchhoff’s diffraction integral (three dimensions) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kirchhoff1883\" data-ref=\"kirchhoff1883\">Kirchhoff 1883</a>]</span>; in the two-dimensional demo e^{ikr}/r becomes e^{i(kr − π/4)}/√(λr)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Fresnel number: w is the half-width (or radius) of the opening, z the distance to the screen."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></munder><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Speed, wavelength and optical path L_opt in a medium."}],"example":{"q":"The demo’s default settings: green light <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>532</mn></mrow></semantics></math> nm, slit width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.8</mn></mrow></semantics></math> μm, screen at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></semantics></math> μm. (a) At what angle is the first dark fringe in the far field? (b) What is the Fresnel number, and should the pattern on the screen look like the far field? (c) What happens with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.4</mn></mrow></semantics></math> μm?","steps":["(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.532</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1.8</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.296</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>17.2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi></mrow></semantics></math>.","(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.81</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.532</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>14</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.11</mn></mrow></semantics></math>. Smaller than 1 but not much smaller: broadly like the far field, with differences in detail (minima not quite zero, slightly shifted).","(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.33</mn><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: no angle satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, so there is no dark fringe; the slit acts like a point source and emits a nearly circular wave."],"a":"Click the “narrow slit” preset and tick “lens focal plane” in the demo: the readouts for the first dark fringe and the Fresnel number show these numbers."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-young1804\" data-ref=\"young1804\">Young 1804</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kirchhoff1883\" data-ref=\"kirchhoff1883\">Kirchhoff 1883</a> · <a href=\"#ref-sommerfeld1896\" data-ref=\"sommerfeld1896\">Sommerfeld 1896</a> · <a href=\"#ref-maiman1960\" data-ref=\"maiman1960\">Maiman 1960</a>"},"interference":{"id":"interference","body":[{"p":"Chapter 1 showed that when two beams superpose an extra interference term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msqrt><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>φ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> appears in the intensity. This chapter is about how to make two steady beams, how to read their phase difference, and how “steady” itself can be made quantitative: coherence. Interference is the most sensitive measuring method in optics; it can detect length changes smaller than an atomic nucleus."},{"h":"Young’s double slit"},{"p":"Two narrow slits a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></semantics></math> apart are lit by the same beam. On a screen far away at distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, the path difference to the two slits is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>d</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>y</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Where it is a whole number of wavelengths the waves add, so bright fringes lie at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></semantics></math> with spacing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. The smaller the slit separation and the longer the wavelength, the wider the fringes. Young used exactly this the other way round, measuring the wavelength of light from the fringe spacing <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-young1804\" data-ref=\"young1804\">Young 1804</a>]</span>: about 0.7 μm for red and 0.4 μm for violet, not far from today’s values."},{"p":"Each slit has a width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> too, so the double-slit pattern is the single-slit diffraction envelope times the two-slit interference fringes. When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is a whole number some interference maxima fall exactly on single-slit minima and vanish: missing orders. In the wave tank’s “missing orders: d = 2a” preset the ±2 orders are gone."},{"h":"Two kinds of interferometer"},{"p":"There are only two ways to obtain two coherent beams. One is <b>wavefront splitting</b>: take two pieces of the same wavefront, as in the double slit, Lloyd’s mirror or Fresnel’s biprism. The other is <b>amplitude splitting</b>: a partly reflecting surface divides the amplitude of one beam into two, which travel separate paths and are recombined, as in thin films and the Michelson, Mach–Zehnder and Fabry–Perot interferometers. The second kind can use large extended sources and so gives much brighter fringes; nearly all precision measurement uses it."},{"h":"Thin films: soap bubbles, oil slicks and antireflection coatings"},{"p":"For a transparent film of thickness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, the light reflected from the lower surface travels an extra <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> compared with that from the upper surface (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is the angle inside the film). One must also watch the signs of the two reflections: reflection from a rarer towards a denser medium has a negative amplitude coefficient, equivalent to an extra half wavelength (the “half-wave loss”). A soap film has air on both sides, so the top reflection carries this minus sign and the bottom one does not; a very thin film (much thinner than a wavelength) therefore reflects almost nothing and looks black, which is why the top of a soap bubble goes black just before it bursts."},{"p":"In white light each wavelength adds or cancels according to its own phase difference, and the colours that survive are the colour of the film, changing band by band with thickness. The strip in the demo is a “soap-film colour chart” from 0 to 1500 nm: grey-white, yellow, magenta and blue at small thickness, paler towards larger thickness, where several wavelengths add at once and mix towards white."},{"p":"An antireflection coating uses the same effect in reverse. On glass of index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> deposit a film with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math> and optical thickness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: both reflections suffer the half-wave loss, the extra path is half a wavelength, and they cancel. MgF₂ (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1.38</mn></mrow></semantics></math>) does not quite meet <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>1.52</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>1.23</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, but it already reduces the reflection at 550 nm from 4.3 % to about 1.3 %. The faint purple sheen on a camera lens is the red and blue left over after green has been removed. Conversely, a stack of alternating high- and low-index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow></semantics></math> layers can reflect more than 99.9 %; laser cavity mirrors are made that way."},{"demo":"film"},{"p":"Press a slightly convex lens onto a flat plate: the air gap between them thickens slowly from the centre outwards, and in monochromatic light one sees concentric bright and dark rings, Newton’s rings; the centre is dark (zero gap, only the half-wave loss remains). The radius of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></semantics></math>-th dark ring is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the radius of curvature of the lens. Optical workshops still use such “test plates” to check that surfaces are flat to better than a tenth of a wavelength."},{"h":"The Michelson interferometer and temporal coherence"},{"p":"A Michelson interferometer splits light with a beam splitter into two perpendicular arms; two mirrors send the beams back and they recombine. Moving one mirror by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> changes the path difference by one wavelength and shifts the fringes by one. In 1887 Michelson and Morley used it to look for the Earth’s motion relative to the “ether”; they expected a shift of 0.4 fringe and saw less than a twentieth of that <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-michelson1887\" data-ref=\"michelson1887\">Michelson &amp; Morley 1887</a>]</span>. This null result later became an important experimental background to special relativity."},{"p":"As the path difference grows the fringes become fainter and finally disappear. The reason is that real light is never strictly monochromatic: for a spectral width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the fringes of the different frequency components drift apart and average out after a path difference of about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. This length is the coherence length, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: about 1 μm for white light, about 8 μm for a green LED, a few centimetres for one line of a sodium lamp, and hundreds of metres for a frequency-stabilised single-mode laser. How the fringe visibility <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> varies with path difference is exactly the Fourier transform of the source spectrum (the Wiener–Khinchin theorem)."},{"demo":"michelson"},{"p":"This turns an interferometer into a spectrometer. Scan one mirror, record the intensity against path difference (the interferogram) and Fourier-transform it: the result is the spectrum, with a resolution of about one over the maximum path difference (in wavenumbers). This is Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR): every wavelength is measured at once (Fellgett’s advantage) and no slit throws light away (Jacquinot’s advantage). Nearly every infrared spectrometer in a chemistry lab today is of this kind; see <a href=\"../spectra/#m-ir\">Spectra Hall · infrared spectroscopy</a>."},{"h":"Spatial coherence"},{"p":"Temporal coherence concerns the phase relation at one point between two times; spatial coherence concerns the phase relation at one time between two points separated sideways. An incoherent source subtending an angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is roughly coherent over a width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>. Van Cittert (1934) and Zernike (1938) showed that the degree of coherence far from a source is the Fourier transform of the source’s intensity distribution <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zernike1938\" data-ref=\"zernike1938\">Zernike 1938</a>]</span>. That is why Young’s light first had to pass a pinhole: it reduces the angle the source subtends, widens the coherence region, and puts both slits inside it."},{"p":"The theorem can also be used backwards: measure how the coherence falls off with the separation of two points and infer the size of the source. In 1920 Michelson and Pease mounted a 6 m interferometer beam on the 2.5 m Mount Wilson telescope and measured the angular diameter of Betelgeuse, about 0.047 arcseconds: the first direct measurement of the size of a star other than the Sun. Very-long-baseline radio interferometry today (the Event Horizon Telescope’s images of black-hole shadows) still works on this principle."},{"p":"In 1956 Hanbury Brown and Twiss discovered that spatial coherence can also be measured without comparing phases at all, just by correlating the intensity fluctuations at two detectors <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hbt1956\" data-ref=\"hbt1956\">Hanbury Brown &amp; Twiss 1956</a>]</span>. This “intensity interferometry” led on to quantum optics: photons of thermal light tend to arrive in clumps (bunching), those of a laser arrive at random, and those of a single-photon source avoid each other (antibunching). In 1987 Hong, Ou and Mandel sent two identical photons into the two sides of a beam splitter at the same time: they always left together on the same side, and the rate of coincidences with one photon on each side fell to near zero <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hom1987\" data-ref=\"hom1987\">Hong et al. 1987</a>]</span>, a two-photon interference that classical wave optics cannot explain."},{"h":"Interference one at a time"},{"p":"Make the light so faint that at most one photon is in the apparatus at a time, expose for long enough, and the fringes still appear (G. I. Taylor’s experiment of 1909). Electrons do the same: in 1961 Jönsson made double- and multi-slit electron interference with fabricated fine slits <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-jonsson1961\" data-ref=\"jonsson1961\">Jönsson 1961</a>]</span>; in 1989 Tonomura and colleagues recorded electrons one at a time through an electron biprism: random dots at first, and after tens of thousands the fringes gradually emerged <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-tonomura1989\" data-ref=\"tonomura1989\">Tonomura et al. 1989</a>]</span>. In 1999 even fullerene molecules C₆₀, sixty carbon atoms each, interfered after passing a grating <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-arndt1999\" data-ref=\"arndt1999\">Arndt et al. 1999</a>]</span>. Each particle “interferes with itself”; where it lands is random, but the probability is set by the wave intensity. That is the Born rule of quantum mechanics; see <a href=\"../quantum/physics/#c-wave\">Quantum physics · the wavefunction</a>."},{"h":"Interferometers today"},{"p":"In September 2015 LIGO’s two Michelson interferometers with 4 km arms (plus Fabry–Perot cavities that lengthen the light path a few hundred times) recorded gravitational waves from the merger of two black holes <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ligo2016\" data-ref=\"ligo2016\">LIGO &amp; Virgo 2016</a>]</span>: a peak strain of about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>21</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, meaning the 4 km arms changed length by about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> m, about a four-hundredth of a proton’s diameter. Optical coherence tomography (OCT) in medicine goes to the other extreme: it deliberately uses broadband light with a coherence length of only a few micrometres, so that only the tissue layer within one coherence length of path balance makes fringes, and the retina can be sectioned optically layer by layer <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-huang1991\" data-ref=\"huang1991\">Huang et al. 1991</a>]</span>."}],"points":[{"h":"Fringe spacing Δy = λL/d","p":"Double-slit fringes are the single-slit envelope times the interference fringes; orders go missing when d/a is an integer. Young used it to measure the wavelength of light.","st":"tested"},{"h":"The half-wave loss decides whether a thin film is bright or black","p":"Reflection from rarer to denser medium flips the sign of the amplitude. Very thin soap films therefore look black; the two reflections of a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow></semantics></math> antireflection coating cancel.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Coherence length ≈ λ²/Δλ","p":"The narrower the spectrum, the larger the path difference over which fringes survive. Visibility against path difference is the Fourier transform of the spectrum: that is how FTIR spectrometers work.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The van Cittert–Zernike theorem","p":"Far-field spatial coherence is the Fourier transform of the source’s intensity distribution. Hence one first “makes” coherent light with a pinhole, and conversely one can measure the angular diameter of stars.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Single particles interfere","p":"Photons, electrons and C₆₀ molecules passing one at a time build up fringes. Where each lands is random; the probability is given by the wave intensity.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Two-photon interference has no classical explanation","p":"The Hong–Ou–Mandel dip: two identical photons entering a beam splitter together always leave together. For classical waves the coincidence dip can reach at most one half.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Interference of macroscopic objects","p":"Molecules used in interference experiments now exceed twenty thousand atomic mass units; whether still larger objects can show interference is one way of testing how quantum mechanics and gravity get along.","st":"frontier"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.8em\" maxsize=\"1.8em\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Double-slit fringe spacing and far-field pattern (single-slit envelope × two-slit fringes)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msqrt><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext>  </mtext><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The condition for a dark reflection when only one reflection has the half-wave loss (left), and the two conditions for a single-layer antireflection coating (right)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi>R</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Radius of the m-th dark Newton ring."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>ν</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Coherence length, coherence time and fringe visibility."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></msubsup><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>σ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>σ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Michelson interferogram is a cosine transform of the spectrum S(σ) (σ is the wavenumber): the basis of Fourier-transform spectroscopy."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>ξ</mi></mstyle><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>ξ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>ξ</mi></mstyle></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The van Cittert–Zernike theorem (one dimension): the degree of coherence is the Fourier transform of the source intensity <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zernike1938\" data-ref=\"zernike1938\">Zernike 1938</a>]</span>."}],"example":{"q":"(a) A He–Ne laser (633 nm) lights a double slit with 0.25 mm separation and the screen is 1.00 m away. What is the fringe spacing? (b) For the sodium D lines at 588.995 nm and 589.592 nm, at what interval of path difference does the visibility in a Michelson interferometer reach its minimum, and how far must the mirror move? (c) Roughly what is the coherence length of a green LED (centre 525 nm, full width at half maximum 35 nm)?","steps":["(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>633</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>1.00</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.25</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2.53</mn></mrow></semantics></math> mm.","(b) The wavenumber difference is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.588995</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.589592</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.7191</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi>μ</mi><msup><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, so the beat period is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>581.7</mn></mrow></semantics></math> μm = 0.582 mm of path; the mirror moves half that, 0.291 mm.","(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>52</mn><msup><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>35</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>7875</mn></mrow></semantics></math> nm ≈ 8 μm."],"a":"The two sodium lines are about 2 : 1 in intensity, so the visibility minimum is not zero but about 1/3; choose the sodium lamp in the Michelson demo to see it. LED fringes are visible only within a few micrometres of path balance, which is exactly why OCT can section tissue."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-young1804\" data-ref=\"young1804\">Young 1804</a> · <a href=\"#ref-michelson1887\" data-ref=\"michelson1887\">Michelson &amp; Morley 1887</a> · <a href=\"#ref-zernike1938\" data-ref=\"zernike1938\">Zernike 1938</a> · <a href=\"#ref-hbt1956\" data-ref=\"hbt1956\">Hanbury Brown &amp; Twiss 1956</a> · <a href=\"#ref-jonsson1961\" data-ref=\"jonsson1961\">Jönsson 1961</a> · <a href=\"#ref-hom1987\" data-ref=\"hom1987\">Hong et al. 1987</a> · <a href=\"#ref-tonomura1989\" data-ref=\"tonomura1989\">Tonomura et al. 1989</a> · <a href=\"#ref-huang1991\" data-ref=\"huang1991\">Huang et al. 1991</a> · <a href=\"#ref-arndt1999\" data-ref=\"arndt1999\">Arndt et al. 1999</a> · <a href=\"#ref-ligo2016\" data-ref=\"ligo2016\">LIGO &amp; Virgo 2016</a>"},"diffraction":{"id":"diffraction","body":[{"p":"When light meets an obstacle or passes an opening it bends into the geometric shadow: that is diffraction. It is not just “slightly blurred edges”: it sets the upper limit on the resolution of every optical instrument, and it underlies gratings, X-ray crystallography and holography. This chapter goes through the most important diffraction patterns, by the shape of the opening and the distance of observation."},{"h":"The Fraunhofer single slit"},{"p":"A slit of width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is lit by parallel light and viewed far away (or in a lens’s focal plane) at angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Divide the slit into halves: corresponding points of the upper and lower halves differ in path by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; when that equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> the two halves cancel pair by pair, giving the first dark fringe, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. In general the minima lie at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>±</mo><mn>2</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>…</mo></mrow></semantics></math>). The full intensity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>β</mi></mrow></semantics></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. The central maximum has a full angular width of about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, twice that of the others; the first subsidiary maximum is only 4.7 % of the central one."},{"p":"Here is a very general “uncertainty relation”: the narrower the opening, the larger the diffraction angle, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Squeezing light into a narrow region costs a spread of directions. It is the same property of the Fourier transform as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>x</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>≳</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> in quantum mechanics: a function and its Fourier transform cannot both be narrow (chapter 8)."},{"h":"Fresnel diffraction and the Cornu spiral"},{"p":"When the screen is not far enough away, the distance from points of the slit to a point on the screen contains a quadratic term, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. Put into the Kirchhoff integral, it gives the Fresnel integrals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Plotted in the complex plane, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> is a spiral winding at its two ends towards <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>±</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mo>±</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>: the Cornu spiral. The amplitude at a point on the screen is the chord between two points of the spiral; this is the same as the phasor diagram of chapter 1, except that the wavelets’ phase grows quadratically, so the small arrows curl round and round."},{"p":"The straight edge is the cleanest case. At the boundary of the geometric shadow the intensity is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow></semantics></math> of the unobstructed value (the chord from the centre of the spiral to one end is half that from end to end); moving into the lit region it oscillates, peaking at about 1.37, and settles towards 1 ever more rapidly; moving into the shadow it falls monotonically to zero. Choose “straight edge” in the demo to see this curve. Each edge of a slit produces such a set of fringes; at large Fresnel number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> they do not disturb each other and one sees the geometric shadow with edge fringes; once <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> drops below 1 the two sets merge into the Fraunhofer pattern."},{"demo":"fresnel"},{"h":"The Poisson spot: a prediction meant to refute a theory"},{"p":"In 1818 the French Academy of Sciences set a prize essay on diffraction, and Fresnel submitted his wave theory. One of the judges, Poisson, a supporter of the corpuscular theory, derived from Fresnel’s equations a seemingly absurd conclusion: there should be a bright spot at the very centre of the shadow of a disc, as bright as with no disc at all, because every point of the disc’s rim is the same distance from the axis and all the wavelets arrive in phase. Another judge, Arago, did the experiment, and the spot was there. Fresnel won, and the spot became known as the Poisson or Arago spot. The wave tank’s “Poisson spot” preset is its two-dimensional version: a bright line along the centre of the obstacle’s shadow."},{"p":"Babinet’s principle connects obstacles and openings: the diffracted fields of a screen and of its complement (openings and opaque parts swapped) add up to the field with nothing in the way. So wherever the unobstructed field is zero (in the far field, every direction except the centre) the two diffraction patterns are identical: a thin wire and a slit of the same width have the same far-field pattern. Measuring the diameter of a hair with a laser pointer relies on this."},{"h":"The round aperture and the Airy disc"},{"p":"Lenses, telescopes and pupils are round. The Fraunhofer pattern of a round aperture of diameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>v</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> the first-order Bessel function. The central bright spot is the Airy disc (computed by Airy in 1835); it holds 83.8 % of the energy, and the first dark ring lies at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.22</mn><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. The factor 1.22 is the first zero of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, 3.8317, divided by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"p":"In 1879, studying spectroscopes, Rayleigh proposed a convention: two points are just resolved when the centre of one Airy disc falls on the first dark ring of the other <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rayleigh1879\" data-ref=\"rayleigh1879\">Rayleigh 1879</a>]</span>. The dip between the peaks is then 73.5 % of the peak height. The smallest resolvable angle of an imaging system is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.22</mn><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: about 46 arcseconds for a 3 mm pupil at 550 nm, about 0.058 arcseconds for the 2.4 m Hubble telescope. This limit does not depend on how well the lenses are made; it is a property of waves themselves."},{"demo":"airy"},{"p":"The microscope version is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.61</mn><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></mrow></semantics></math> (for self-luminous points such as fluorescent molecules), where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is the numerical aperture of the objective, at most about 1.4 for oil immersion, so roughly 200 nm with visible light. Under coherent illumination the limit comes from Abbe’s theory and the numbers differ slightly (chapter 8). The super-resolution microscopy that beats this limit does not overturn diffraction; it sidesteps the assumption that one records a single image, as chapter 8 explains."},{"h":"Zone plates and “diffraction-free” beams"},{"p":"Fresnel divided a round opening into rings such that neighbouring zones differ in path to a point on the axis by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; the outer radius of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math>-th zone is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>. Neighbouring zones contribute with opposite signs and cancel; block the even zones and the rest all arrive in phase, so that point on the axis becomes far brighter. That is the Fresnel zone plate, a “lens” that focuses by diffraction rather than refraction. Glass can barely refract X-rays, so the focusing elements in synchrotron beamlines and X-ray microscopes are zone plates."},{"p":"Every beam of finite width spreads by diffraction, but in 1987 Durnin and colleagues made a beam that keeps its width over a long distance <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-durnin1987\" data-ref=\"durnin1987\">Durnin et al. 1987</a>]</span>: its cross-section is the zero-order Bessel function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, formed by superposing plane waves arriving on a cone. “Diffraction-free” has a price: each ring of side lobes carries as much energy as the centre, so an ideal Bessel beam would need infinite power; what can actually be made is only approximately unchanged over a finite stretch."},{"note":"<b>How exact diffraction theory is.</b> The formulas of this chapter come from scalar, paraxial or far-field approximations. When the opening is comparable to the wavelength, at large angles, or when polarisation matters, one must return to Maxwell’s equations: Sommerfeld gave an exact solution for a perfectly conducting half-plane in 1896 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-sommerfeld1896\" data-ref=\"sommerfeld1896\">Sommerfeld 1896</a>]</span>, and nowadays one usually solves numerically with methods such as finite-difference time-domain (FDTD).","kind":"info"}],"points":[{"h":"Single-slit minima a sin θ = mλ","p":"The central maximum is 2λ/a wide and the subsidiary maxima only 4.7 %. The narrower the opening, the larger the angle: Δθ ≈ λ/a and Δx Δp ≳ ħ are the same Fourier property.","st":"proved"},{"h":"At a straight edge the intensity is 1/4","p":"At the boundary of the geometric shadow the intensity is exactly a quarter of the unobstructed value; in the lit region it peaks at about 1.37.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The Poisson spot","p":"In 1818 Poisson meant it to refute the wave theory; Arago’s experiment confirmed it. There is a bright spot in the centre of a disc’s shadow.","st":"history"},{"h":"The Rayleigh criterion θ = 1.22 λ/D","p":"The dip between the peaks is 73.5 %. It is a convention, not a law of physics; but the order of magnitude λ/D cannot be escaped by any single image.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Babinet’s principle","p":"Complementary screens give identical far-field patterns away from the centre: a wire and a slit of the same width look the same.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Zone plates focus by diffraction","p":"Zone radii <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>. The focusing elements of X-ray microscopes and synchrotron beamlines.","st":"tested"},{"h":"“Diffraction-free” only over a finite distance","p":"The core of a Bessel beam keeps its width over a long distance, but the energy is spread over countless rings; any real beam of finite aperture eventually spreads.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>β</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>a</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Fraunhofer single slit."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>u</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>π</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>t</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">∣</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">∣</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Fresnel integrals and Fresnel diffraction by a slit (1 = unobstructed), u = √(2/(λz))·(slit edge − x)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>J</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mi>v</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>D</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.22</mn><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mi>D</mi></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Airy pattern of a round aperture and its first dark ring."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.22</mn><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mi>D</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.61</mn><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Rayleigh criterion: telescope (angle) and microscope (distance in the object plane) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rayleigh1879\" data-ref=\"rayleigh1879\">Rayleigh 1879</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Outer radius of the n-th zone of a Fresnel zone plate (f is the focal length)."}],"example":{"q":"(a) A slit 0.10 mm wide is lit by a 633 nm laser and the screen is 2.0 m away. How wide is the central maximum, and is the pattern Fraunhofer? (b) With a 3 mm pupil and light of 550 nm, beyond what distance can one no longer separate the two headlights of a car, 1.4 m apart, at night?","steps":["(a) Full width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>633</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow></semantics></math> mm.","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>633</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mo>×</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2.0</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≪</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math>: Fraunhofer.","(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.22</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>550</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>3</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2.24</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> rad (46″).","Distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1.4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2.24</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>6.3</mn></mrow></semantics></math> km."],"a":"This is the upper limit set by diffraction. In practice the spacing of the retina’s photoreceptors, the eye’s aberrations and atmospheric shimmer make it somewhat shorter. Set the aperture to 3 mm in the Airy demo and the Rayleigh limit reads 46 arcseconds."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-rayleigh1879\" data-ref=\"rayleigh1879\">Rayleigh 1879</a> · <a href=\"#ref-sommerfeld1896\" data-ref=\"sommerfeld1896\">Sommerfeld 1896</a> · <a href=\"#ref-durnin1987\" data-ref=\"durnin1987\">Durnin et al. 1987</a>"},"gratings":{"id":"gratings","body":[{"p":"The fringes of a double slit are broad and soft, half bright and half dark. With more slits the pattern changes character: the bright fringes become ever narrower and brighter, and almost everything between them is dark. That is because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> equal small arrows form a straight line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> times as long, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> times the intensity of one slit, only when every arrow points the same way (the path difference between neighbouring slits a whole number of wavelengths); slightly off, the arrows close into a polygon. A grating carries this to the extreme: tens of thousands of slits, with bright fringes narrow enough to separate two wavelengths differing by a part in a thousand."},{"h":"N slits"},{"p":"For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> slits of width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and spacing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></semantics></math> the far-field intensity is the single-slit factor times the multi-slit factor: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>β</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>π</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. The multi-slit factor equals 1 where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: the principal maxima. Between two principal maxima there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> zeros and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math> subsidiary maxima, the strongest of which tends to about 4.7 % of a principal maximum as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> grows. A principal maximum has a half-width of about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>: the more slits, the narrower. The single-slit factor only governs the relative brightness of the orders; a missing order is one of its zeros landing on a principal maximum."},{"h":"The grating equation and resolving power"},{"p":"Light incident at angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> on a grating of period <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is diffracted into order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></semantics></math> in the direction satisfying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> is the undispersed straight-through beam (or specular reflection); in the others different wavelengths are turned through different angles, with angular dispersion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>. The denser the ruling and the higher the order, the farther apart the colours."},{"p":"Large dispersion is not enough to separate lines: two lines must be farther apart than their own widths. By the Rayleigh criterion (the maximum of one line on the first zero of the other) the smallest resolvable wavelength difference is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, that is, the resolving power <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rayleigh1879\" data-ref=\"rayleigh1879\">Rayleigh 1879</a>]</span>. It depends only on the order and the total number of illuminated slits, not on the spacing. The two sodium D lines are 0.597 nm apart, so in first order one needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>589.3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.597</mn><mo>≈</mo><mn>990</mn></mrow></semantics></math> slits; in the demo you can watch them split from one blob into two as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> goes from 500 to 2000."},{"demo":"grating"},{"p":"Higher orders give higher resolving power, but with a catch: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> in order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> in order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></semantics></math> land at the same angle and overlap. The range of wavelengths free of overlap is the free spectral range, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. Ordinary spectrometers therefore work in first order with a filter to block second-order light (otherwise 400 nm in second order mixes with 800 nm in first); echelle gratings use orders from tens to over a hundred and separate the orders with a prism in the perpendicular direction, laying the spectrum out as a two-dimensional image."},{"h":"Blaze: herding light into the order you want"},{"p":"A simple transmission grating wastes most of its energy in the zero order (which disperses nothing). A reflection grating can shape each groove into a small facet whose specular direction coincides with the diffraction direction of one order (say m = 1), so that order receives 70–90 % of the light near the design wavelength. This is a blazed grating, and the facet tilt is the blaze angle. Adjusting “slit width / period” in the demo is the same idea in another guise: the single-slit envelope decides how the energy is shared among the orders."},{"h":"History: from fine wires to ruling engines"},{"p":"In 1814 Fraunhofer saw and catalogued hundreds of dark lines in the solar spectrum, later understood as absorption lines of atoms in the Sun and in the Earth’s atmosphere (the sodium D lines carry his letter “D”). In 1821 he wound fine wires into a grating and for the first time measured absolute wavelengths of spectral lines with one. What really transformed spectroscopy were the ruling engines of the late nineteenth century: Rowland’s machine at Johns Hopkins used a precision screw to rule more than fourteen thousand lines per inch on speculum metal, and he invented the concave grating, ruled on a sphere so that it forms an image by itself without lenses <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rowland1882\" data-ref=\"rowland1882\">Rowland 1882</a>]</span>. Most atomic spectroscopy of the following decades came from gratings of this kind."},{"p":"Most gratings today are holographic (photoresist exposed to the fringes of two interfering laser beams) or replicated from a master. A periodic index modulation written into an optical fibre is a fibre Bragg grating: it reflects only a narrow band and serves as a filter in telecommunications and as a sensor of strain and temperature; it works like the Bragg reflection in crystals of chapter 5."},{"h":"Prisms and gratings"},{"p":"A prism separates colours by dispersion (the index varies with wavelength); its resolving power is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is the length of the prism’s base. A 5 cm flint-glass prism has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math> of a few thousand in the visible; a 5 cm grating with 600 lines per millimetre has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>000</mn></mrow></semantics></math> in first order. A prism’s advantages are a single “order” with no overlap and high transmission; its drawbacks are uneven dispersion (the blue end far more spread out than the red) and the need for different materials in the ultraviolet and infrared."},{"h":"Other uses of gratings"},{"p":"Two parallel gratings send different colours along paths of different length, so they can stretch or compress an ultrashort pulse. In 1985 Strickland and Mourou first stretched a pulse to lower its peak power, amplified it, and finally squeezed it back with a grating pair <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-strickland1985\" data-ref=\"strickland1985\">Strickland &amp; Mourou 1985</a>]</span>: chirped-pulse amplification (CPA). It brings tabletop lasers to terawatt peak powers and above; today’s eye surgery and attosecond science both rely on it."},{"p":"“Gratings” are everywhere in nature and daily life: the data tracks of an optical disc are 1.6 μm (CD) or 0.74 μm (DVD) apart and show rainbows when tilted; ridges a few hundred nanometres apart on the wing scales of Morpho butterflies give a blue that needs no pigment (structural colour). The ultraviolet–visible, fluorescence, Raman and atomic-emission spectrometers of a chemistry lab all have at their heart a grating plus an array detector; see <a href=\"../spectra/#m-uvvis\">Spectra Hall · UV–visible</a> and <a href=\"../spectra/#m-raman\">Raman</a>."}],"points":[{"h":"Principal maxima: intensity ∝ N², width ∝ 1/N","p":"Between principal maxima there are N − 1 zeros and N − 2 subsidiary maxima; energy is conserved, so the brighter the fringes the narrower.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The grating equation d(sin θ_m − sin θ_i) = mλ","p":"The period alone sets the directions; the shape of the slits only affects the brightness of the orders.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Resolving power R = mN","p":"Independent of the spacing; only the order and the number of illuminated slits matter. Separating the sodium doublet needs about 990 slits in first order.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Free spectral range λ/m","p":"The higher the order, the more easily orders overlap; echelle spectrometers separate them with a prism in the other direction.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Blazing concentrates energy into one order","p":"Grooves shaped as facets aim specular reflection along the chosen order, with efficiencies of 70–90 %.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Rowland’s concave grating","p":"A grating ruled on a sphere forms its own image without lenses; the workhorse of atomic spectroscopy in the late nineteenth century.","st":"history"},{"h":"Chirped-pulse amplification","p":"Grating pairs stretch and recompress ultrashort pulses, raising peak powers by orders of magnitude (Nobel Prize in Physics 2018).","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>β</mi><msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>π</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Fraunhofer pattern of N slits (normalised to 1 at the centre)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The grating equation and angular dispersion."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>N</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Resolving power and free spectral range <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rayleigh1879\" data-ref=\"rayleigh1879\">Rayleigh 1879</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mtext>prism</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>b</mi><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Resolving power of a prism (b is the base length)."}],"example":{"q":"A grating with 600 lines per millimetre is illuminated over 5.0 cm at normal incidence. (a) What is the first-order angle for sodium light at 589.3 nm, and how many orders can be seen at most? (b) What are the first-order resolving power and the smallest resolvable wavelength difference? (c) Starting from 400 nm, where does the first-order free spectral range end?","steps":["(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>600</mn></mrow></semantics></math> mm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1.667</mn></mrow></semantics></math> μm. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.5893</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1.667</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.354</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>20.7</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi></mrow></semantics></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2.83</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, so at most 2 orders on each side.","(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>000</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>000</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>589.3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>30</mn><mtext> </mtext><mn>000</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.020</mn></mrow></semantics></math> nm.","(c) 400 nm in second order coincides with 800 nm in first order, so first order is overlap-free from 400 to 800 nm."],"a":"This grating separates the sodium doublet with plenty to spare (it needs R of only about 1000); in a real spectrometer resolution is often limited by the entrance slit and the detector pixels, short of mN."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-rayleigh1879\" data-ref=\"rayleigh1879\">Rayleigh 1879</a> · <a href=\"#ref-rowland1882\" data-ref=\"rowland1882\">Rowland 1882</a> · <a href=\"#ref-strickland1985\" data-ref=\"strickland1985\">Strickland &amp; Mourou 1985</a>"},"xray":{"id":"xray","body":[{"p":"A grating for visible light has a period of micrometres, matched to visible wavelengths. To see atoms by diffraction one needs a grating with a period of a few tenths of a nanometre and a wavelength just as short. Nature supplies the first ready-made: atoms in a crystal sit in regular rows about 0.1 to 1 nm apart. The second is X-rays: the Kα line from a copper target has a wavelength of 1.5406 Å (0.154 nm). This chapter is the three-dimensional version of chapter 4."},{"h":"1912: X-rays are waves and crystals are lattices"},{"p":"In 1912 nobody knew whether X-rays were waves or particles, nor whether the atoms in a crystal really formed a lattice. Laue saw that if both were true, a crystal should diffract X-rays like a three-dimensional grating. Friedrich and Knipping put a copper sulphate crystal in an X-ray beam, and regularly arranged spots appeared on the photographic plate <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-laue1913\" data-ref=\"laue1913\">Friedrich, Knipping &amp; Laue 1913</a>]</span>. One photograph proved both things at once."},{"p":"Laue explained the spots with grating equations along three directions, all to be satisfied at once, which is rather involved. A few months later the young W. L. Bragg gave a far simpler picture: think of the crystal as a stack of parallel planes of atoms spaced <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></semantics></math> apart, each a very thin half-silvered mirror reflecting a little of the X-rays; the reflections from neighbouring planes differ in path by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is the glancing angle to the planes), and only when that is a whole number of wavelengths do the reflections from all planes add in phase <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-bragg1913\" data-ref=\"bragg1913\">Bragg &amp; Bragg 1913</a>]</span>:"},{"math":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Bragg’s law. Note that θ is measured from the planes; the angle between the diffracted and incident beams is 2θ."},{"p":"The Braggs promptly used their own X-ray spectrometer to determine the structures of NaCl, KCl and diamond: the first time anyone knew how atoms are actually arranged in a solid. There is no “NaCl molecule” in rock salt at all; each Na⁺ is surrounded by six Cl⁻, a conclusion that made some chemists of the time uncomfortable. The demo below breaks Bragg reflection into one small arrow per plane: at the Bragg angle the arrows line up, slightly off they curl into a circle."},{"demo":"bragg"},{"h":"The reciprocal lattice and the Ewald sphere"},{"p":"The more general statement is the Laue condition: the difference between the incident and diffracted wavevectors <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, without the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow></semantics></math> by crystallographers’ convention) must equal a vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> of the reciprocal lattice. The reciprocal lattice is spanned by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo>×</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>V</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and two similar vectors; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, and its direction is perpendicular to the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> planes. The Laue condition and Bragg’s law are the same thing: taking the modulus gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"p":"Ewald gave it a geometric picture: draw a sphere of radius 1/λ with the tip of the incident wavevector at the origin of the reciprocal lattice. Whichever reciprocal-lattice point lies exactly on the sphere gives a diffracted beam in that direction. A monochromatic beam on a stationary single crystal generally meets few points, so one rotates the crystal (rotation and four-circle methods), gives the sphere a thickness with a continuous X-ray spectrum (the Laue method), or uses countless randomly oriented grains (the powder method)."},{"h":"The structure factor: atomic positions hidden in peak intensities"},{"p":"The size and shape of the unit cell decide only the directions of diffraction; how the atoms sit inside the cell decides how strong each direction is. The amplitude of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> reflection is the structure factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math>, summed over the atoms of the cell, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> is the atomic scattering factor (roughly proportional to the number of electrons, falling as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> grows) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> are fractional coordinates. The intensity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∝</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>F</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>."},{"p":"With symmetry some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></semantics></math> vanish identically: systematic absences, the fingerprints of the lattice type. A body-centred lattice (atoms at 0,0,0 and ½,½,½) requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></semantics></math> even; a face-centred lattice requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></semantics></math> all odd or all even; the diamond structure further removes those with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, so silicon has no (200). In the rock-salt structure the all-odd peaks go as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>: NaCl’s (111) is weak, and in KCl, where K⁺ and Cl⁻ both have 18 electrons, (111) all but vanishes and the pattern looks like a simple cubic cell of half the edge. That is how the Braggs recognised the two as the same structure."},{"h":"Powder diffraction"},{"p":"Grind a crystal to powder and for every family of planes some grains sit exactly at the Bragg angle; the diffracted beams form cones (Debye–Scherrer rings). Sweep a detector through <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and one gets a pattern of peaks: positions give the unit cell, intensities the atomic positions, widths the crystallite size and strain. Every crystalline phase has its own pattern, like a fingerprint, so powder diffraction is the first choice for identifying solid phases, and databases hold hundreds of thousands of reference patterns."},{"p":"When crystallites are small, only a limited number of planes take part and the peaks broaden (the effect of reducing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> in the first demo). The Scherrer formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> relates the peak width (in radians) to the crystallite size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; Patterson discussed carefully in 1939 in what sense it holds <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-patterson1939\" data-ref=\"patterson1939\">Patterson 1939</a>]</span>. In 1969 Rietveld proposed no longer separating peaks one by one but fitting the whole pattern directly with a model of the crystal structure <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rietveld1969\" data-ref=\"rietveld1969\">Rietveld 1969</a>]</span>; this is today’s standard method of powder structure refinement."},{"demo":"powder"},{"h":"The phase problem"},{"p":"If every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></semantics></math> were known (modulus and phase), one inverse Fourier transform would give the electron density in the cell, with atoms at its peaks. The trouble is that detectors record only intensities <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>F</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>; the phases are lost. This is the phase problem, and it kept crystal-structure determination a craft for decades. In 1934 Patterson found that Fourier-transforming <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>F</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> directly, without phases, gives the autocorrelation of the electron density, whose peaks sit at the vectors between atoms <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-patterson1934\" data-ref=\"patterson1934\">Patterson 1934</a>]</span>; vectors between heavy atoms stand out and can be fixed first. The later “direct methods” derive phases from intensities by statistical relations (Nobel Prize in Chemistry 1985), and small-molecule structures can now be solved almost automatically. For proteins phases usually come from heavy-atom substitution or anomalous scattering."},{"h":"DNA, proteins and quasicrystals"},{"p":"Diffraction does not need a perfect three-dimensional crystal. In 1953 Franklin and Gosling photographed the diffraction of fibres of B-form DNA, a striking “X” pattern: the diffraction of a helix is a cross, the layer-line spacing gives the 3.4 nm pitch and the strong meridional spots the 0.34 nm spacing of base pairs <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-franklin1953\" data-ref=\"franklin1953\">Franklin &amp; Gosling 1953</a>]</span>. It appeared in the same issue of Nature as Watson and Crick’s model. In 1958 Kendrew and colleagues gave the first three-dimensional structure of a protein (myoglobin) from X-ray diffraction <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kendrew1958\" data-ref=\"kendrew1958\">Kendrew et al. 1958</a>]</span>; most of the more than two hundred thousand structures in today’s Protein Data Bank come from X-ray crystallography."},{"p":"In 1982 Shechtman saw, by electron diffraction, that an aluminium–manganese alloy gave sharp spots arranged with tenfold symmetry <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-shechtman1984\" data-ref=\"shechtman1984\">Shechtman et al. 1984</a>]</span>. Sharp spots mean long-range order, yet fivefold and tenfold symmetry cannot coexist with translational periodicity: this was a solid that is ordered but not periodic, a quasicrystal. The International Union of Crystallography later redefined a “crystal” as a solid whose diffraction pattern is essentially discrete, and Shechtman received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 2011."},{"h":"Diffraction without crystals: X-ray lasers"},{"p":"The diffraction of a single molecule is too weak; to amplify the signal one has to line countless molecules up in a crystal. In 2000 simulations by Neutze and colleagues proposed another way: use extremely intense X-ray pulses only a few femtoseconds long and record the diffraction before the molecule is blown apart (“diffraction before destruction”) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-neutze2000\" data-ref=\"neutze2000\">Neutze et al. 2000</a>]</span>. Once X-ray free-electron lasers were built, Chapman and colleagues in 2011 collected diffraction patterns from hundreds of thousands of nanocrystals one at a time and assembled the structure of photosystem I <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-chapman2011\" data-ref=\"chapman2011\">Chapman et al. 2011</a>]</span>. Separately, Miao and colleagues showed in 1999 that if the diffraction pattern is sampled more finely than the Bragg condition requires (oversampling), even non-crystalline samples can have their phases recovered by iterative algorithms and an image reconstructed <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-miao1999\" data-ref=\"miao1999\">Miao et al. 1999</a>]</span>: coherent diffraction imaging, taken up again in chapter 8."},{"p":"Electrons and neutrons diffract too. Electrons interact strongly with matter and suit thin samples and surfaces (and cryo-electron microscopy); neutrons are sensitive to light atoms (hydrogen) and to magnetic moments. All three speak the same language of Bragg’s law and structure factors. The chapter <a href=\"../chemistry/structural/#c-diffraction\">Structural chemistry · diffraction and structure solution</a> in the Chemistry Disciplines covers direct methods, refinement and R factors; the Spectra Hall explains how to read <a href=\"../spectra/#m-pxrd\">powder</a> and <a href=\"../spectra/#m-scxrd\">single-crystal</a> diffraction data."}],"points":[{"h":"One photograph of 1912 proved two things","p":"X-rays are waves, and the atoms of a crystal form a lattice. Laue won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1914 and the Braggs in 1915.","st":"history"},{"h":"Bragg’s law nλ = 2d sin θ","p":"Equivalent to the Laue condition k′ − k = G; the Ewald sphere turns it into geometry.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Positions give the cell, intensities the atoms","p":"The structure factor F_hkl is the coherent sum of scattering by the atoms of the cell; systematic absences are fingerprints of the lattice type.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Scherrer: peak width ∝ 1/crystallite size","p":"β = Kλ/(L cos θ), K ≈ 0.9. Valid only for crystallites below about 100 nm; strain and instrumental broadening widen peaks too.","st":"approx"},{"h":"The phase problem","p":"Detectors record only |F|²; the Patterson function, direct methods, heavy atoms and anomalous scattering are ways round it.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Quasicrystals: ordered but not periodic","p":"Sharp diffraction spots with tenfold symmetry; the definition of a “crystal” was changed to “essentially discrete diffraction”.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Diffraction before destruction","p":"Femtosecond X-ray pulses record diffraction before the sample is destroyed; structure determination of single molecules is still being developed.","st":"frontier"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">k</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">a</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">b</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">c</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Laue condition and the reciprocal lattice (crystallographers’ convention, |k| = 1/λ)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>a</mi><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mtext>(cubic)</mtext></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Plane spacing in the cubic system."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></munder><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>λ</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>h</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>l</mi><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∝</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>2</mn><mi>θ</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>θ</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The structure factor (with the Debye–Waller factor) and powder intensity: multiplicity × |F|² × Lorentz–polarisation factor."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>K</mi><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>K</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The Scherrer formula: β is the full width at half maximum (in 2θ, radians) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-patterson1939\" data-ref=\"patterson1939\">Patterson 1939</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>V</mi></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi></mrow></msup><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>V</mi></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mtext> </mtext><msub><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">G</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⋅</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The electron density is a Fourier series of the structure factors; the Patterson function uses only intensities and peaks at interatomic vectors <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-patterson1934\" data-ref=\"patterson1934\">Patterson 1934</a>]</span>."}],"example":{"q":"NaCl has lattice parameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5.640</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å; use Cu Kα₁ (1.5406 Å). (a) At what <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is the (200) peak? (b) Why is there no (100) peak? (c) If the crystallites are only 20 nm, what is the Scherrer width of (200)?","steps":["(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>200</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5.640</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>2.820</mn></mrow></semantics></math> Å, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5406</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>2.820</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.2732</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15.85</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>31.70</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi></mrow></semantics></math>.","(b) Rock salt is face-centred; (100) has mixed odd and even indices, so the contributions of the four face-centred positions cancel and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>100</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>.","(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1.5406</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>200</mn><mtext> </mtext><mover accent=\"true\"><mtext>A</mtext><mo>˚</mo></mover><mo>×</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>15.85</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>7.21</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> rad <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.41</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi></mrow></semantics></math>."],"a":"Choose sodium chloride in the powder demo and set the crystallites to 20 nm: (200) sits at 31.70° in the peak table and the readout gives the Scherrer width of the first peak. A laboratory diffractometer’s instrumental broadening is usually 0.05–0.1°, smaller than this, so 20 nm crystallites are easy to spot from the peak widths."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-bragg1913\" data-ref=\"bragg1913\">Bragg &amp; Bragg 1913</a> · <a href=\"#ref-laue1913\" data-ref=\"laue1913\">Friedrich, Knipping &amp; Laue 1913</a> · <a href=\"#ref-patterson1934\" data-ref=\"patterson1934\">Patterson 1934</a> · <a href=\"#ref-patterson1939\" data-ref=\"patterson1939\">Patterson 1939</a> · <a href=\"#ref-franklin1953\" data-ref=\"franklin1953\">Franklin &amp; Gosling 1953</a> · <a href=\"#ref-kendrew1958\" data-ref=\"kendrew1958\">Kendrew et al. 1958</a> · <a href=\"#ref-rietveld1969\" data-ref=\"rietveld1969\">Rietveld 1969</a> · <a href=\"#ref-shechtman1984\" data-ref=\"shechtman1984\">Shechtman et al. 1984</a> · <a href=\"#ref-miao1999\" data-ref=\"miao1999\">Miao et al. 1999</a> · <a href=\"#ref-neutze2000\" data-ref=\"neutze2000\">Neutze et al. 2000</a> · <a href=\"#ref-chapman2011\" data-ref=\"chapman2011\">Chapman et al. 2011</a>"},"polarization":{"id":"polarization","body":[{"p":"The first five chapters used only a scalar “size of the wave”. That is enough for interference and diffraction, but it leaves something out: the electric field of light is a vector, perpendicular to the direction of travel. For light moving along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, the field can oscillate along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, or along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, or any combination. Which way it oscillates is the polarisation. Sound is a longitudinal wave and has none; light has it, which in itself proves that light is transverse."},{"h":"At an interface: Fresnel’s equations"},{"p":"Light passes from a medium of index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> into one of index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>; the plane of incidence contains the incident ray and the surface normal. The component of the electric field perpendicular to that plane is called s, the component in it p. Maxwell’s equations require the tangential components of the electric and magnetic fields to be continuous across the boundary, which gives two reflection amplitudes: Fresnel’s equations. At normal incidence the two agree, and air to glass (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></semantics></math>) reflects <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; at oblique incidence the s reflection keeps growing, while the p reflection first falls and reaches zero at one particular angle."},{"p":"That angle is Brewster’s angle, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>: 56.3° from air to glass, 53.1° from air to water. Brewster found it experimentally in 1815 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-brewster1815\" data-ref=\"brewster1815\">Brewster 1815</a>]</span>. The geometric explanation is that the refracted ray is then exactly perpendicular to the reflected direction, so the dipoles set oscillating in the medium point along the reflected direction, and a dipole does not radiate along its own axis. So near Brewster’s angle the glare from water and roads is almost entirely horizontally polarised, and polarised sunglasses, which pass only vertical polarisation, cut it out. Photographers’ polarising filters and the Brewster windows set at an angle on the ends of laser tubes rely on the same thing."},{"p":"Going the other way, from a denser to a rarer medium (glass to air), beyond the critical angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math> (41.8° for glass) there is no refracted light and everything is reflected. This is why an optical fibre can trap light in its core for tens of kilometres (chapter 7). Under total internal reflection there is still an evanescent wave on the other side, travelling along the surface and decaying exponentially outwards over about a wavelength; total-internal-reflection fluorescence microscopy (TIRF), attenuated-total-reflection infrared (ATR) and surface-plasmon sensors all look only at this thin layer."},{"demo":"reflect"},{"h":"Jones vectors: the algebra of polarisation"},{"p":"Monochromatic, fully polarised light can be written as two complex numbers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>, whose moduli are the amplitudes along the two directions and whose difference of arguments is the phase difference between the components. With a phase difference of 0 or π the field goes back and forth along a line: linear polarisation; with ±π/2 and equal amplitudes the tip of the field traces a circle: circular polarisation; otherwise it is elliptical. In 1941 Jones wrote every optical element as a 2 × 2 matrix <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-jones1941\" data-ref=\"jones1941\">Jones 1941</a>]</span>: a polariser is a projection, a wave plate introduces a fixed phase difference between its fast and slow axes (π/2 for a quarter-wave plate, π for a half-wave plate), and a train of elements is a product of matrices."},{"p":"The most entertaining result is the “three polarisers”: two crossed polarisers block all the light, yet slip a third at 45° between them and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>45</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>45</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow></semantics></math> gets through. The middle one does not “let something through”; it re-projects the polarisation onto a new direction. Malus’s law <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is simply the square of a projection. Partially polarised and unpolarised light need Stokes parameters or Mueller matrices; Jones vectors describe only fully polarised light."},{"demo":"jones"},{"h":"Birefringence"},{"p":"Calcite, quartz, liquid crystals and stretched plastics have different refractive indices for different polarisations: birefringence. Look at print through calcite and you see two images; put a stressed plastic ruler between crossed polarisers and coloured stress fringes appear (photoelasticity), which engineers use to see the stress in a part. A wave plate is just a birefringent crystal cut to the thickness that makes the path difference between the two polarisations exactly λ/4 or λ/2. Each pixel of a liquid-crystal display is a voltage-controlled wave plate between two polarisers: adjusting the voltage adjusts the brightness."},{"h":"Chirality: optical rotation and circular dichroism"},{"p":"Linear polarisation can be regarded as equal amounts of left and right circular polarisation. A chiral molecule (one not superimposable on its mirror image) has slightly different refractive indices for the two circular polarisations; after the sample the phase between them has changed and the resulting linear polarisation is rotated: optical rotation, which is how Pasteur separated the two mirror-image crystals of tartaric acid in 1848. If the molecule absorbs left and right circular polarisation differently, that is circular dichroism (CD): a protein’s α-helix gives two negative bands at 208 nm and 222 nm and a β-sheet one near 218 nm, so a CD spectrum is the quickest way to estimate secondary structure. The Spectra Hall explains how to read <a href=\"../spectra/#m-cd\">circular dichroism</a> and <a href=\"../spectra/#m-polarimetry\">polarimetry</a>."},{"h":"The blue and the polarisation of the sky"},{"p":"Particles much smaller than the wavelength (air molecules) are driven by the electric field of the light and become oscillating dipoles that radiate in all directions. The power of dipole radiation goes as the fourth power of frequency, so the scattered intensity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∝</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>: blue light (450 nm) is scattered about 5.9 times more than red (700 nm), and the sky is blue and sunsets are red. Rayleigh explained the colour of the sky this way in 1871 <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rayleigh1871\" data-ref=\"rayleigh1871\">Rayleigh 1871</a>]</span>. A dipole does not radiate along its axis, so at 90° from the Sun skylight is strongly polarised (up to 100 % in the ideal case, about 70 % in practice), and bees and some birds can see this and use it to navigate."},{"p":"When particles are about as large as the wavelength, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> no longer holds and one must solve Maxwell’s equations in full for a sphere, which Mie did in 1908 to explain the colour of colloidal gold <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-mie1908\" data-ref=\"mie1908\">Mie 1908</a>]</span>. Cloud droplets are a few micrometres across and scatter all colours about equally, so clouds are white; the localised surface plasmon of gold nanoparticles makes them absorb strongly near 520 nm, and the solution is wine-red. Dynamic light scattering (DLS) measures particle size from fluctuations of the scattered intensity; see <a href=\"../spectra/#m-dls\">dynamic light scattering</a> in the Spectra Hall."}],"points":[{"h":"Polarisation proves light is transverse","p":"The electric field is perpendicular to the direction of travel; s and p are the two components relative to the plane of incidence.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Normal-incidence reflectance ((n − 1)/(n + 1))²","p":"Glass 4 %, water 2 %, diamond 17 %; an uncoated lens loses this much at every surface.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Brewster’s angle tan θ_B = n₂/n₁","p":"p-polarised light is not reflected at all, which is why glare from water is horizontally polarised.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Total internal reflection and the evanescent wave","p":"Beyond the critical angle everything is reflected, but an evanescent field about a wavelength deep lies outside; TIRF and ATR rely on it.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Three polarisers pass 1/8","p":"A polariser is a projection; multiplying Jones matrices handles any train of ideal elements.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Optical rotation and CD see chirality","p":"Different refractive indices or absorptions for left and right circular polarisation; CD spectra estimate protein secondary structure.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Rayleigh scattering ∝ 1/λ⁴","p":"Holds only for particles much smaller than the wavelength; larger particles need Mie theory.","st":"approx"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>r</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Fresnel’s equations (non-magnetic media), with θ_t from Snell’s law."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>tan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Brewster’s angle and the critical angle."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>θ</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi></mrow></msup></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Jones matrices: a polariser with axis at θ, and a wave plate with fast axis at θ and retardance Γ (π/2 for quarter-wave, π for half-wave) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-jones1941\" data-ref=\"jones1941\">Jones 1941</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Malus’s law."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mfrac><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Rayleigh scattering of unpolarised light: α is the polarisability and Θ the scattering angle <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rayleigh1871\" data-ref=\"rayleigh1871\">Rayleigh 1871</a>]</span>."}],"example":{"q":"Unpolarised light falls from air onto water (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.333</mn></mrow></semantics></math>). (a) What is Brewster’s angle? (b) How much is reflected at normal incidence? (c) At Brewster’s angle, how much of the s polarisation is reflected?","steps":["(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>arctan</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>1.333</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>53.12</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi></mrow></semantics></math>.","(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.333</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2.333</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>2.04</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math>.","(c) At Brewster’s angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>90</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>36.88</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>53.12</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1.333</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>36.88</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>53.12</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1.333</mn><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>36.88</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.600</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1.066</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0.600</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1.066</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>0.280</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>7.8</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">%</mi></mrow></semantics></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math>."],"a":"About 3.9 % of the unpolarised light (half of 7.8 %) is reflected, all of it horizontally polarised. Choose “Air → water” in the Fresnel demo and press “Brewster’s angle” to see it."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-brewster1815\" data-ref=\"brewster1815\">Brewster 1815</a> · <a href=\"#ref-rayleigh1871\" data-ref=\"rayleigh1871\">Rayleigh 1871</a> · <a href=\"#ref-mie1908\" data-ref=\"mie1908\">Mie 1908</a> · <a href=\"#ref-jones1941\" data-ref=\"jones1941\">Jones 1941</a>"},"geometric":{"id":"geometric","body":[{"p":"The previous chapters kept saying “light is a wave”. Yet in everyday life sunlight through a gap in the curtains is a straight shaft, and spectacles, cameras and microscopes are all designed with rays. There is no contradiction: when every obstacle and opening is much larger than the wavelength, the diffraction angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></semantics></math> goes to zero, every patch of the wavefront goes its own way perpendicular to the wavefront, and that path is a ray. Geometric optics is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> limit of wave optics, just as classical mechanics is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></semantics></math> limit of quantum mechanics (mathematically the same procedure, the eikonal or WKB approximation)."},{"h":"Fermat’s principle and Snell’s law"},{"p":"Fermat’s principle says that the path light takes from one point to another makes the optical path length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mi>n</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></semantics></math> stationary (usually a minimum). In a uniform medium that is a straight line; at a boundary between two media it gives Snell’s law <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>: light is slower in the denser medium, so it prefers to travel less of its way there. From the wave point of view Fermat’s principle says exactly what chapter 1 said: only near the path of stationary optical length do neighbouring paths have nearly the same phase, so their wavelets add constructively; the contributions of all other paths cancel."},{"h":"Lenses and imaging"},{"p":"A convex lens brings parallel light to a focus. In the paraxial approximation (rays at small angles to the axis, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>θ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>) the object distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math>, image distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></semantics></math> and focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></semantics></math> satisfy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and the focal length of a thin lens follows from the radii of curvature of its two faces and its index (the lensmaker’s formula). This paraxial theory (also called Gaussian optics) can be written with 2 × 2 ray-transfer matrices, a train of lenses being a product of matrices, formally just like the Jones matrices of the previous chapter."},{"p":"Real rays, however, are not all close to the axis. Apply Snell’s law exactly at every surface for every ray (ray tracing) and one finds that a spherical lens does not focus to a point: rays farther from the axis focus closer to the lens. This is spherical aberration. It grows as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></semantics></math> is the ray height), so halving the aperture cuts the longitudinal aberration to a quarter. The demo below traces every ray exactly. For the same plano-convex lens, with the curved side facing the parallel beam the two surfaces share the bending and the aberration is about four times smaller than with the flat side towards the beam; that is the first rule of thumb in photography and telescopes about which way round a lens goes."},{"demo":"lens"},{"p":"Spherical aberration is only one of the five monochromatic aberrations Seidel classified; the others are coma, astigmatism, field curvature and distortion. The index also changes with wavelength (dispersion), so blue light has a shorter focal length than red: chromatic aberration. Cementing a convex crown-glass lens to a concave flint-glass lens (an achromatic doublet) brings two colours to the same focus. Modern microscope objectives and camera lenses contain a dozen or more elements, all optimised by computers tracing millions of rays. Aspheric lenses (today made by moulding or diamond turning) can remove spherical aberration with a single surface."},{"h":"Numerical aperture: where geometry meets waves"},{"p":"An objective accepts a cone of light of half-angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; its numerical aperture is defined as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></semantics></math> being the index of the medium between object and objective). NA governs two things at once: how much light is collected (brightness goes as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math>) and how fine a structure can be resolved (about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.61</mn><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>, the Airy disc of chapter 3). A dry objective cannot exceed NA 1; with immersion oil (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.52</mn></mrow></semantics></math>) between sample and objective NA can exceed 1.4, which is the point of oil-immersion lenses. A system whose aberrations are all corrected so that only diffraction limits it is called “diffraction-limited”: geometric design ends there, and what follows belongs to the wave optics of chapter 8."},{"h":"Optical fibres"},{"p":"An optical fibre has a core of slightly higher index than its cladding; light entering at a small angle keeps undergoing total internal reflection at the core–cladding boundary and is trapped in the core. In 1966 Kao and Hockham pointed out that the thousand-plus dB per kilometre lost in the glasses of the day came mainly from impurities, not from glass itself, and that with the impurities removed the loss could fall below 20 dB/km, making fibres usable for communication <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-kao1966\" data-ref=\"kao1966\">Kao &amp; Hockham 1966</a>]</span>. A few years later Corning achieved it; today’s silica fibres lose about 0.2 dB/km at 1550 nm, close to the floor set by Rayleigh scattering (chapter 6). Kao received the 2009 Nobel Prize in Physics for this."},{"h":"Optical tweezers: holding things with light"},{"p":"Light carries momentum; when a ray is refracted by a small transparent bead its direction changes and the bead feels the reaction. When a high-NA objective focuses a laser to a very sharp point, a bead of higher index than its surroundings is drawn towards the brightest spot and held stably near the focus. In 1986 Ashkin and colleagues achieved this three-dimensionally stable optical trap with a single laser beam <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-ashkin1986\" data-ref=\"ashkin1986\">Ashkin et al. 1986</a>]</span>; it was later used to hold living bacteria and viruses and to measure that kinesin walks in 8 nm steps with forces of a few piconewtons. Ashkin received the 2018 Nobel Prize in Physics. For beads larger than the wavelength the force can be computed by ray tracing; for particles smaller than the wavelength one uses the force on a dipole in a field gradient, once again the border between the geometric and the wave pictures."}],"points":[{"h":"Geometric optics is the λ → 0 limit","p":"Valid when obstacles are much larger than the wavelength; it fails near foci, shadow edges and small openings.","st":"approx"},{"h":"Fermat’s principle ⇔ Snell’s law","p":"The optical path is stationary; in wave terms, wavelets near the stationary-phase path add constructively.","st":"proved"},{"h":"Spherical aberration ∝ h²","p":"For a plano-convex lens, curved side towards the parallel beam gives about four times less aberration.","st":"proved"},{"h":"NA governs both brightness and resolution","p":"NA = n sin α; oil-immersion objectives exceed 1.4.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Fibres: impurities were the main loss","p":"Predicted in 1966; about 0.2 dB/km today, close to the Rayleigh-scattering floor.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Optical tweezers hold particles with light’s momentum","p":"A single tightly focused laser beam traps in three dimensions; it measured the steps and forces of molecular motors.","st":"tested"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mtext> ⁣</mtext><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msubsup><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold\">r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1em\"/><mo>⟹</mo><mspace width=\"1em\"/><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Fermat’s principle and Snell’s law."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>o</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>f</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>f</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The thin-lens imaging equation and the lensmaker’s formula (paraxial, in air)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msup><mi>y</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><msup><mi>u</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>f</mi></mrow></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>d</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>0</mn></mstyle></mtd><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mtable rowspacing=\"0.16em\" columnalign=\"center\" columnspacing=\"1em\"><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>y</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mi>u</mi></mstyle></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Ray-transfer matrices: travel a distance d, then a thin lens of focal length f (y is height, u is angle)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>α</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>arcsin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Numerical aperture; the acceptance angle of a fibre."}],"example":{"q":"A plano-convex lens, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.5168</mn></mrow></semantics></math> (BK7), radius of curvature 50 mm. (a) What is its focal length in the thin-lens approximation? (b) For a single-mode fibre with core <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.468</mn></mrow></semantics></math> and cladding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.462</mn></mrow></semantics></math>, what are the numerical aperture and acceptance angle?","steps":["(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1.5168</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>50</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0.010336</mn></mrow></semantics></math> mm⁻¹, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>96.75</mn></mrow></semantics></math> mm.","(b) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1.46</mn><msup><mn>8</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1.46</mn><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>0.017580</mn></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1326</mn></mrow></semantics></math>; the acceptance angle is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>arcsin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>0.1326</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>7.6</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">°</mi></mrow></semantics></math>."],"a":"The ray-tracing demo shows the lensmaker’s value of 96.75 mm in its readout; the paraxial focus of the real 8 mm thick lens (measured from the first vertex) lies slightly beyond it, and the marginal rays clearly closer: that is the spherical aberration."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-kao1966\" data-ref=\"kao1966\">Kao &amp; Hockham 1966</a> · <a href=\"#ref-ashkin1986\" data-ref=\"ashkin1986\">Ashkin et al. 1986</a>"},"fourier":{"id":"fourier","body":[{"p":"Chapter 3 noted that the Fraunhofer diffraction pattern is the Fourier transform of the aperture, only one has to be “at infinity” to see it. A converging lens brings infinity to its back focal plane: with the object in the front focal plane, the field in the back focal plane is exactly the object’s Fourier transform, each point corresponding to a spatial frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi></mrow></semantics></math> being the focal length). A second lens transforms again and gives the image. This is the 4f system, the basic apparatus of Fourier optics: by intervening in the plane between the two lenses one chooses which spatial frequencies the image keeps."},{"h":"Abbe: why the microscope has a limit"},{"p":"In 1873, while working with Zeiss, Abbe proposed that the microscope image must be understood in two steps <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-abbe1873\" data-ref=\"abbe1873\">Abbe 1873</a>]</span>. The object (say a grating of period <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></semantics></math>) first diffracts the illumination into the zeroth, ±1st, ±2nd orders and so on, at angles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; the objective can catch only those orders with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>; and the image is the interference of the orders that were caught. If only the zeroth order is caught, the image is a uniform glow and the fringes vanish entirely; one needs at least the zeroth and one first order to see fringes. So with coherent normal illumination the smallest resolvable period is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></mrow></semantics></math>, improved to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></semantics></math> with oblique illumination. That is the resolution limit of the microscope: not a badly ground lens, but diffracted light carrying the detail that never enters the objective."},{"demo":"abbe"},{"h":"Seeing transparent things: dark field and phase contrast"},{"p":"A living cell is almost transparent: it changes the phase of light slightly (more optical path where it is thicker) and leaves the amplitude nearly untouched. Eyes and cameras see only intensity, so in an ordinary microscope it is nearly invisible. Block the zeroth order in the back focal plane (dark field) and the background goes black; only scattered light forms the image, and edges light up. Zernike thought more cleverly: for a weak phase object <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, the scattered light is exactly 90° out of step with the zeroth order, so the intensity has no first-order term in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>; shift the zeroth order by another 90° (a phase plate) and the two parts are in or out of step, the intensity becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>, and phase differences turn directly into light and dark <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zernike1942\" data-ref=\"zernike1942\">Zernike 1942</a>]</span>. Zernike received the 1953 Nobel Prize in Physics. In today’s biology laboratories phase contrast and its relative, differential interference contrast (DIC), are in use almost daily."},{"h":"Holography: recording the phase as well"},{"p":"A photographic plate records only intensity and loses the phase, the same trouble as the phase problem of X-ray diffraction in chapter 5. In 1948, trying to improve the electron microscope, Gabor found a way: let the light from the object interfere with a known reference beam, and the interference fringes store both the amplitude and the phase of the object wave; illuminate the plate again with the reference beam and the object wavefront is reconstructed <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gabor1948\" data-ref=\"gabor1948\">Gabor 1948</a>]</span>. This is holography, which became truly practical only after the laser (Gabor won the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1971). Today’s digital holographic microscopes record the interferogram directly on a camera and reconstruct the phase by Fourier transform in a computer."},{"h":"The fast Fourier transform"},{"p":"The discrete Fourier transform of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> samples needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></semantics></math> multiplications by definition; the algorithm Cooley and Tukey published in 1965 needs about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><msub><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>N</mi></mrow></semantics></math> <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-cooley1965\" data-ref=\"cooley1965\">Cooley &amp; Tukey 1965</a>]</span>, fifty thousand times faster for a million points. Without it Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (chapter 2), NMR, digital holography, the demo above and most of today’s signal processing could not run in real time. Every demo in this section is computed live in the browser; the Abbe imaging one does an FFT of the object, blocks part of the spectrum in the frequency domain, and does an inverse FFT."},{"h":"Getting round the diffraction limit: super-resolution fluorescence microscopy"},{"p":"Abbe’s limit is strict for “light collected by a lens”. But a fluorescence microscope looks not at scattered light but at light emitted by the molecules themselves, and molecules can be switched on and off. In 1994 Hell and Wichmann proposed STED: a ring-shaped “stimulated emission depletion” beam forces the molecules in the outer part of the excitation spot back to the ground state, leaving only a central point far smaller than the diffraction limit to fluoresce <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-hell1994\" data-ref=\"hell1994\">Hell &amp; Wichmann 1994</a>]</span>. In 2006 another route appeared almost simultaneously: let only a sparse few molecules light up at random each time; their images are still diffraction spots, but the centre of an isolated spot can be located to a few nanometres, and repeating this thousands of times builds up the picture (PALM <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-betzig2006\" data-ref=\"betzig2006\">Betzig et al. 2006</a>]</span>, STORM <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-rust2006\" data-ref=\"rust2006\">Rust et al. 2006</a>]</span>). Both push resolution to around 20 nm, and Betzig, Hell and Moerner shared the 2014 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. They did not overturn Abbe: the diffraction spot is as large as ever; the trick is that neighbouring molecules do not emit at the same moment."},{"h":"Imaging without lenses"},{"p":"Good X-ray lenses are almost impossible to make. In 1999 Miao and colleagues showed that if the diffraction pattern of an isolated small object is sampled finely enough (at least twice as finely as the Bragg condition requires: oversampling), then under the constraint that “outside the object there is nothing”, an iterative algorithm projecting back and forth between real and frequency space recovers the lost phases <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-miao1999\" data-ref=\"miao1999\">Miao et al. 1999</a>]</span>. This is coherent diffraction imaging: a computer takes the place of the objective. Combined with the X-ray free-electron lasers of chapter 5 it is pushing imaging towards single nanoparticles, viruses and cells."},{"h":"Engineered optical materials"},{"p":"In 1987 Yablonovitch and John independently proposed that a structure whose refractive index varies periodically, with a period comparable to the wavelength, gives photons bands and band gaps just as a crystal does for electrons: light in some range of frequencies cannot propagate in any direction <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-yablonovitch1987\" data-ref=\"yablonovitch1987\">Yablonovitch 1987</a>; <a href=\"#ref-john1987\" data-ref=\"john1987\">John 1987</a>]</span>. These are photonic crystals; the Bragg mirror of chapter 2 is the one-dimensional version, and the structural colours of butterflies and peacock feathers are nature’s. In 2000 Pendry pointed out that a material of refractive index −1 could make a “perfect lens” that even amplifies evanescent waves, in principle beating the diffraction limit <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-pendry2000\" data-ref=\"pendry2000\">Pendry 2000</a>]</span>; in 2001 Shelby and colleagues observed negative refraction at microwave frequencies in a metamaterial of metal rings and wires <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-shelby2001\" data-ref=\"shelby2001\">Shelby et al. 2001</a>]</span>. Low-loss negative-index materials at visible wavelengths remain difficult."},{"p":"Light can also carry orbital angular momentum. In 1992 Allen and colleagues pointed out that a beam whose wavefront is helical, with phase varying around the axis as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></semantics></math> (a Laguerre–Gaussian mode), carries <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℓ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ℏ</mi></mrow></semantics></math> of orbital angular momentum per photon, distinct from spin (the ±ħ of circular polarisation) <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-allen1992\" data-ref=\"allen1992\">Allen et al. 1992</a>]</span>. Such “vortex beams” are dark at the centre, can set particles in optical tweezers spinning, and are being studied for multiplexing information in a single fibre. The side of light that comes as individual photons (photon counting, entanglement, the HOM effect) is covered in the <a href=\"../quantum/physics/#c-optics\">quantum optics</a> chapter of the Quantum Physics pavilion."},{"h":"Open questions"},{"list":["Low-loss negative-index materials at visible wavelengths, and a real “perfect lens”, have not been made; absorption in metals is the main obstacle.","X-ray imaging of single biological macromolecules (without crystals) is still developing: the signal is very weak, orientations are random and radiation damage is fast; so far only viruses and larger particles have been imaged, at modest resolution.","Super-resolution microscopy in living cells still trades speed, phototoxicity and labelling density: higher resolution usually means slower imaging and more damage.","How far photonic crystals and metasurfaces can replace conventional bulk lenses and prisms, especially over broad bands with high efficiency, is not settled."]}],"points":[{"h":"A lens performs a Fourier transform","p":"The back focal plane holds the spatial spectrum of the object; a 4f system can filter in the plane between the lenses.","st":"proved"},{"h":"The Abbe limit λ/NA","p":"Without the first diffraction order, that period cannot be seen; oblique illumination improves this to λ/(2NA).","st":"proved"},{"h":"Phase contrast turns phase into brightness","p":"Shift the zero order by 90° and a weak phase object’s intensity becomes 1 ± 2φ.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Holography records amplitude and phase","p":"Interference with a known reference beam; digital holography reconstructs in the computer.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Super-resolution: not emitting at the same time","p":"STED depletes the rim, PALM/STORM localise one by one; about 20 nm, without overturning Abbe.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Oversampling recovers the phase","p":"If the diffraction pattern of an isolated object is sampled finely enough, iterative algorithms recover the phase.","st":"tested"},{"h":"Photonic crystals and metamaterials","p":"Photonic band gaps have been realised in several bands; negative refraction in the visible is still limited by loss.","st":"frontier"}],"math":[{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>λ</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>∫</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mi>F</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The field in a lens’s back focal plane is the Fourier transform of the object in its front focal plane (scalar, paraxial)."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><mi>λ</mi></mfrac><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></mfrac><mtext> (coherent, normal)</mtext><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mfrac><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mtext> (oblique)</mtext></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"The cut-off frequency of coherent imaging and the Abbe limit <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-abbe1873\" data-ref=\"abbe1873\">Abbe 1873</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>ϕ</mi><mtext> </mtext><mover><mo stretchy=\"true\" minsize=\"3.0em\">→</mo><mpadded width=\"+0.6em\" lspace=\"0.3em\"><mrow><mtext> zero order</mtext><mo>×</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>π</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext></mrow></mpadded></mover><mtext> </mtext><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mi>I</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Zernike phase contrast: the first-order term of a weak phase object becomes intensity <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-zernike1942\" data-ref=\"zernike1942\">Zernike 1942</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>O</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>R</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>O</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>R</mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>O</mi><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>O</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>R</mi></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Holography: the cross term O R* stores both the amplitude and phase of the object wave <span class=\"chm-cites\">[<a href=\"#ref-gabor1948\" data-ref=\"gabor1948\">Gabor 1948</a>]</span>."},{"m":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><msqrt><mi>N</mi></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></semantics></math>","cap":"Single-molecule localisation precision: s is the width of the diffraction spot and N the number of photons collected (ignoring background and pixelation)."}],"example":{"q":"Green light (550 nm), an objective of NA 0.4, coherent normal illumination. (a) What is the smallest resolvable grating period? (b) For gratings of period 1.2 μm and 1.4 μm, which diffraction orders get in? (c) What about an oil objective of NA 1.4?","steps":["(a) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.55</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>0.4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1.375</mn></mrow></semantics></math> μm.","(b) The first order has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>λ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>p</mi></mrow></semantics></math>: 0.458 &gt; 0.4 for 1.2 μm, so it misses and the image has no fringes; 0.393 &lt; 0.4 for 1.4 μm, so the ±1 orders just get in and fringes appear.","(c) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.55</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>1.4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0.39</mn></mrow></semantics></math> μm, three and a half times better."],"a":"The two preset buttons in the Abbe imaging demo are exactly the two cases of (b): “Orders passed” changes from “zero order only” to “0, ±1”, and the image contrast jumps up from 0."},"cites":"<a href=\"#ref-abbe1873\" data-ref=\"abbe1873\">Abbe 1873</a> · <a href=\"#ref-zernike1942\" data-ref=\"zernike1942\">Zernike 1942</a> · <a href=\"#ref-gabor1948\" data-ref=\"gabor1948\">Gabor 1948</a> · <a href=\"#ref-cooley1965\" data-ref=\"cooley1965\">Cooley &amp; Tukey 1965</a> · <a href=\"#ref-john1987\" data-ref=\"john1987\">John 1987</a> · <a href=\"#ref-yablonovitch1987\" data-ref=\"yablonovitch1987\">Yablonovitch 1987</a> · <a href=\"#ref-allen1992\" data-ref=\"allen1992\">Allen et al. 1992</a> · <a href=\"#ref-hell1994\" data-ref=\"hell1994\">Hell &amp; Wichmann 1994</a> · <a href=\"#ref-miao1999\" data-ref=\"miao1999\">Miao et al. 1999</a> · <a href=\"#ref-pendry2000\" data-ref=\"pendry2000\">Pendry 2000</a> · <a href=\"#ref-shelby2001\" data-ref=\"shelby2001\">Shelby et al. 2001</a> · <a href=\"#ref-betzig2006\" data-ref=\"betzig2006\">Betzig et al. 2006</a> · <a href=\"#ref-rust2006\" data-ref=\"rust2006\">Rust et al. 2006</a>"}}};
